(window.PHASE_DATA=window.PHASE_DATA||{})[1]={"pages": [{"pdf": 28, "printed": 1, "ch": 0, "text": "PHASE 1\n\nFoundations of Uncertainty, Money\nand Finance\nChapters 1–6\n\nKnight (1921); Keynes (1936, 1937); a short, removable chapter on Minsky"}, {"pdf": 29, "printed": 2, "ch": 1, "text": "CHAPTER 1\n\nOrientation: Why These Readings, and\nthe Minsky Question\n1.1 What Phase 1 is for\nYour thesis asks whether banks, and the stress tests that supervisors build for them, can handle climate change. Before any model is estimated, an examiner will ask a plainer question: what kind of ignorance are we dealing with? If the future of a bank’s loan book under a net-zero transition could be described by a known probability distribution, then a Value-at-Risk number would settle the matter. If it cannot, then every number in the thesis is conditional on a story, and the thesis needs a way of saying so honestly. Phase 1 gives you that language. It has four short readings:\n• Knight (1921), Chapters VII and VIII: the original distinction between risk (a known distribution) and uncertainty (no usable distribution), and an account of how business institutions have grown up around that difference.\n• Keynes (1937): the same idea applied to money, interest and investment. Because the future is unknowable, people lean on conventions, and conventions can break suddenly.\n• Minsky (1992): a financial-instability theory built on Keynes. It is the reading the thesis has been built around until now, and the one we are reconsidering (next section). Together they make one chain of thought. Knight says why the future cannot be priced. Keynes says what people do instead, and why that makes investment and asset prices unstable. Minsky says how that instability builds up inside the debt structure of an economy. You can enter the chain at any point, and the notes are written so that Chapter 5 (Minsky) can be removed without breaking Chapters 2 to 4.\n\n1.2 The Minsky question\nYou asked me to recall why the thesis is moving away from Minsky. I have to be straightforward about what I can and cannot recall. The thesis notes record that, as of today, you want to concentrate on the larger thesis rather than the 31 October TIES deadline, and that you are considering dropping the Grey firm category and possibly the Minsky component. You asked to see a lean-Minsky version and a no-Minsky version before deciding. The notes do not record a final decision, and they do not record your reasons in your own words. What I can do is lay out, from the thesis files, the pressures that are visible. These are my inference, not your stated reasons:"}, {"pdf": 30, "printed": 3, "ch": 1, "text": "Chapter\n\nSource\n\nCentral question\n\nLength of the original\n\nKnight (1921), Ch. VII\n\nWhat is the difference be- 36 printed pages (pp.\ntween risk and uncertainty? 197–232)\n\nKnight (1921), Ch. VIII\n\nWhat institutions arise to 31 printed pages (pp.\ndeal with uncertainty?\n233–263)\n\nKeynes (1937)\n\nHow\ndoes\nuncertainty 15 pages (pp. 209–223)\nshape money, interest and\ninvestment?\n\nMinsky (1992)\n\nHow does debt build insta- about 8 pages\nbility?\n\nSynthesis\n\nHow do the three fit, and —\nwhat do they give the thesis?\n\nTable 1.1. The chapters of Phase 1.\n\n1. The data link is unfinished. The planned Minsky interaction term (climate exposure multiplied by firm leverage) needs firm-level leverage from CMIE ProwessIQ, which has not yet been integrated.\n2. The eclectic framework is hard to defend. The thesis combines Post-Keynesian instability theory with neoclassical information economics (Stiglitz–Weiss). The notes already flag that this needs explicit justification, and it is an obvious line of attack in a viva.\n3. Minsky’s own mechanism does not map neatly onto a climate shock. Minsky\nsays that his hypothesis “does not rely upon exogenous shocks to generate business cycles” (Minsky 1992, p. 8). A climate-policy shock is partly exogenous. Chapter 5 shows how to handle this, but it is a real tension.\n4. The core contributions do not strictly need it. The Emerging Market Blind Spot argument and the Green/Brown taxonomy can stand without a Minsky Moment. Chapter 5 sets out what the thesis would lose and what it could use in its place (Table 5.3). Please read it as input to your decision, not as a recommendation to drop or keep. If your real reasons differ from the four above, tell me and I will adjust the chapter. How the notes handle the open decision Chapters 2 to 4 (Knight and Keynes) are written to stand on their own and are needed under either option. Chapter 5 (Minsky) is written as a self-contained, removable chapter. If you drop Minsky, skip Chapter 5 and Section 6.3; nothing else depends on them.\n\n1.3 A short primer for this phase\nThe readings in this phase use no mathematics beyond the ideas below. If they are familiar, skip this section."}, {"pdf": 31, "printed": 4, "ch": 1, "text": "Probability and expected value\nA probability is a number between 0 and 1 that says how often an outcome occurs in the long run, or how strongly you believe it will. The expected value of a gamble is each outcome multiplied by its probability, added up. A bet that pays 100 rupees with probability 0.05 and nothing otherwise has an expected value of 0.05 × 100 = 5 rupees. If a bank makes a thousand loans, each with a 5% chance of a 100-rupee loss, it can expect to lose about 5,000 rupees in total, whichever particular loans go bad.\n\nThe law of large numbers\nWhen many similar events are added together, the average result settles down towards the expected value. A single loan either defaults or it does not; a pool of ten thousand similar loans loses very close to 5% of its value, year after year. Knight’s whole discussion of insurance rests on this idea, which he calls grouping or consolidation. The key assumption is that the events are alike and do not move together.\n\nIndependence and correlation\nTwo risks are independent if one happening tells you nothing about the other (two coin tosses). They are correlated if they tend to happen together (two factories in the same flooded valley). Pooling removes the risk of independent events. It does not remove the risk that comes from a shock hitting all of them at once. Figure 3.2 in Chapter 3 shows this; it matters a great deal for climate risk.\n\nDistribution, tail, and measuring risk\nA distribution lists the possible outcomes and how likely each is. The tail is the far end, where rare, very bad outcomes sit. Value-at-Risk (VaR) asks: “what loss will we not exceed in 95% (or 99%) of cases?” Conditional Value-at-Risk (CVaR) asks: “if we do land in the worst 5%, how bad is it on average?” Both need a distribution. Knight’s question is whether, for the decisions that matter most, such a distribution exists at all. (Phase 5 treats VaR and CVaR properly.)\n\nObjective and subjective probability\nAn objective probability comes from the world: the six faces of a fair die, or a mortality table built from millions of lives. A subjective probability is a person’s degree of confidence, which may rest on little or nothing. Knight’s terms for the two are risk and uncertainty.\n\n1.4 How to study and teach from these notes\nA suggested rhythm for each chapter: read the chapter once without stopping; close it and write down, from memory, the three or four ideas that carry the argument; attempt the recall and explain questions; read the thesis-link section and decide whether you agree with each suggestion; only then try the viva questions and compare with the answer sketches. To teach"}, {"pdf": 32, "printed": 5, "ch": 1, "text": "from a chapter, use the lecture outline in Chapter 6 (Table 6.3), which converts the four readings into one 90-minute session with timings and board work.\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ1.1 In one sentence each, say what Knight, Keynes and Minsky contribute to the chain of thought described in Section 1.1.\nQ1.2 Name two ways in which a pool of loans can behave differently from a pool of independent coin tosses. Explain\nQ1.3 Why does the law of large numbers make an insurer’s business predictable but a single firm’s decision not? Apply\nQ1.4 Write down three decisions a public sector bank makes that you think have a usable history of similar cases, and three that do not. Keep the list; you will revisit it after Chapter 3. Viva\nQ1.5 An examiner asks: “You say climate risk is uncertain, not risky. Then why does your thesis compute probabilities and CVaR?” Draft a two-sentence answer now, and compare it with your answer after Chapter 2.\n\nAnswer sketches\nA1.1 Knight: the future cannot be priced for decisions that are unique; the economy’s institutions are shaped by that. Keynes: people therefore rely on conventions, which makes money demand, interest rates and investment unstable. Minsky: debt commitments turn that instability into a self-reinforcing cycle.\nA1.2 Correlation (loans move together under a common shock) and dependence on unknown or changing probabilities (the default rate itself is not known and may change). Either one leaves a risk that pooling cannot remove.\nA1.3 The insurer faces many alike cases and can estimate frequencies, so the law of large numbers works. A firm facing a unique choice has no group of similar cases to average over.\nA1.4 Expect a mix. For example, with history: retail loan delinquencies, working-capital loans to stable firms. Without: lending to a new green-hydrogen venture, or the effect of a future domestic carbon price.\nA1.5 A strong answer separates the question (“what is the true probability?”) from the use (“what loss do we hold capital for?”). Scenarios and CVaR are used as stress-testing devices conditional on stated stories, not as claims about objective probability."}, {"pdf": 33, "printed": 6, "ch": 2, "text": "CHAPTER 2\n\nKnight (1921), Chapter VII: The Meaning of Risk and Uncertainty\nSource line\nFrank H. Knight, Risk, Uncertainty, and Profit (Boston: Houghton Mifflin, 1921), Chapter VII, “The Meaning of Risk and Uncertainty”, printed pp. 197–232. Your file contains the chapter as text. Teaching notes in your folder (Knight_Ch7_Teaching_Notes.pdf) cover the same ground more briefly; where they differ from the original, this chapter follows the original.\n\n2.1 Where the chapter sits and what it is for\nKnight’s book is a theory of profit. In the chapters before this one he has built the standard picture of perfect competition, in which every firm earns exactly what its inputs cost and nobody makes a pure profit. That picture needs a drastic assumption, which he states at the opening of Chapter VII: “the assumption of practical omniscience on the part of every member of the competitive system” (p. 197). Everyone knows what will happen. Profit exists in the real world because that assumption is false. So the chapter does what the first paragraph promises: “take a brief excursion into the field of the theory of knowledge” to ask what imperfect knowledge actually means, and what it does to economic behaviour. The result is the most-cited distinction in the economics of uncertainty. But the way Knight arrives at it, through a rather philosophical road, is worth following. It explains why he defines uncertainty as he does, and it will help you defend the distinction when an examiner pushes.\n\n2.2 Why it is uncertainty, not change, that matters\nThe first move is to separate two things that are easily confused: change, and ignorance of change. Entrepreneurs hire labour and buy materials at agreed prices now, and sell the product later. Profit arises when the later selling price differs from what the earlier costs implied. But if changes followed known laws, they could be foreseen, costs would already reflect them, and no gap would open. Hence the sentence the teaching notes rightly quote: “it is our imperfect knowledge of the future, a consequence of change, not change as such, which is crucial for the understanding of our problem” (p. 198). In plain words: a bakery\n\nA baker buys flour in January at a price agreed in advance and sells bread in March. If everyone knows in January exactly what flour and bread will cost in March, the flour price"}, {"pdf": 34, "printed": 7, "ch": 2, "text": "already includes that knowledge and the baker earns only the normal reward for baking. Profit or loss comes from being surprised: the March price differs from what the January agreement assumed. The change in prices is not the cause. The surprise is. Knight then makes a second, subtler point at the bottom of p. 198 and top of p. 199. Knowing the individual outcome in advance is not necessary. It is enough to know all the possible outcomes and the probability of each. A businessman who cannot predict one venture can still, “by figuring on the basis of a large number of ventures,” turn losses “into fixed costs” (p. 199). Such costs “would be costs merely, like any other necessary outlays, and would not give rise to profit.” This is the seed of everything that follows. Some uncertainty can be neutralised by arithmetic; if all uncertainty could be, profit would again vanish. So the question becomes: which kinds of ignorance can be neutralised, and which cannot?\n\n2.3 Knowledge, consciousness and action\nTo answer, Knight builds a short theory of how people act on knowledge. You do not need to agree with his philosophy of mind, and he is careful to say that he takes no position on the ultimate nature of reality (footnote, pp. 200–201). What you need is the functional claim.\n\nMentality is forward-looking\n“The essence of mentality from a functional standpoint seems to be its forward-looking character” (p. 200). A plant adjusts to its environment as the environment changes. An animal, by contrast, “can react to a situation before that situation materializes; it can ‘see things coming.’ ” The further ahead an organism can see, the better it can adapt. Economic life is the human version of this: we produce today for wants that exist tomorrow.\n\nWe react to what we infer, not to what we perceive\n“We perceive the world before we react to it, and we react not to what we perceive, but always to what we infer” (p. 201). Behaviour that is aimed at the future has three parts: perceiving the present, inferring what the future would be without our action, and inferring what our action will change. Knight then lists four places where this can go wrong (p. 202). They are the kernel of his argument that perfect foresight is impossible, so it is worth having them in a table.\n\n2.4 The “dogma of uniformity”: why a finite mind can cope at all\nIf the world were infinitely varied and every object unique, no finite mind could reason about it. Knight asks what has to be true for thought to work, and answers with what he calls a dogma: “the world is made up of things, which, under the same circumstances, always behave in the same way” (p. 204). That is not enough, because there are too many things. We also need classes: kinds of things that behave alike. But exhaustive classification would give “a quite impossible number of kinds” (p. 206), so we use shifting groupings by purpose, treating things as"}, {"pdf": 35, "printed": 8, "ch": 2, "text": "Source of error\n\nEveryday example\n\nWe do not perceive the present ac- A lender sees a borrower’s balance sheet but not the curately or completely. plant’s real condition.\n\nWe do not infer the future from Last year’s loan growth is projected forward, but a policy the present with dependable accu- change alters the trend. racy.\n\nWe do not accurately know the A bank tightens credit to one sector and cannot say how consequences of our own actions. its borrowers will respond.\n\nWe do not carry out actions in ex- A credit policy is approved at headquarters and applied actly the form we intend. differently in branches.\n\nTable 2.1. Knight’s four sources of error (p. 202), with illustrations.\n\nalike in one respect for one problem and in another respect for another. His seven propositions (pp. 207–208) summarise the conditions: the world is practically infinite in variety, yet finite intelligence copes because the number of distinguishable properties is limited, properties are fairly constant, they change in fairly constant ways, and non-visible properties go with visible ones in fairly uniform ways. In plain words\n\nReasoning about the future is only possible because the world repeats itself enough, and because we can sort things into groups that behave alike. When a situation is new enough that no group of “cases like this one” exists, the machinery of inference has nothing to work on. That is where uncertainty, in Knight’s sense, begins. Knight adds a warning that matters for a thesis on modelling. The logic taught to students is a logic of exact knowledge, of rigorous demonstration (p. 209). But “the ordinary decisions of life are made on the basis of ‘estimates’ of a crude and superficial character” (p. 210), and only “in very special and crucial cases” can a mathematical, exhaustive study be made (pp. 210–211). When people decide, they usually do not reason step by step. They reach a view by what he calls “judgment,” “common sense,” or “intuition” (p. 211). He quotes Marshall in a footnote that business managers are guided by “trained instinct” rather than knowledge.\n\n2.5 From inference to probability\nNot all inference is certain inference. We often know only that some X are Y: that in ninety per cent of cases a thing of type X is also Y (p. 212). That is a probability judgment, and if the proportion is known it can guide conduct “much the same as if the association were universal.” Knight’s example is champagne: a known share of bottles burst, so the loss is a fixed cost of the business. And insurance is the same idea at work: “No one can say whether a particular building will burn,” but by pooling many owners the contingency becomes a fixed cost (pp. 213). The step that matters comes next. Knight insists on “a further distinction … of far-reaching consequences and much neglected in the discussion of economic problems” (p. 214). There are two fundamentally different ways of arriving at a probability:"}, {"pdf": 36, "printed": 9, "ch": 2, "text": "• A priori. You can calculate it from the structure of the problem, as with a perfect die: “it would be merely ridiculous to undertake to throw it a few hundred thousand times” to find out (p. 215).\n• Statistical. You cannot calculate it; you must count. Nobody can compute from first principles the proportion of buildings that will burn; “it must be dealt with, if at all, by tabulating the results of experience” (p. 215). Knight stresses that in business the first type is “practically never met with,” while the second is “extremely common” (p. 215). He also notes that statistical probability is never exact (you would need infinitely many cases) and that it depends on how alike the grouped cases are.\n\nThe homogeneity trap\nKnight spends a page (pp. 217–218) on a statistical warning that is still taught today. American soldiers in the Philippines had a lower death rate than the general population in the United States. Did that show the Philippines were healthier? No: the general population is a mixture of children, the old, the sick, while soldiers are young and fit. The groups are not homogeneous, so the comparison misleads. He then draws out a paradox: if you could group cases so perfectly that they were absolutely alike, nature’s uniformity would imply identical results for every one of them, and “the idea of probability becomes meaningless.” Probability only exists where the grouped cases differ in ways we cannot see or control. The practical point: how good a probability estimate is depends on how well you can classify the cases, and classification is always imperfect. You can skim this part\n\nPages 218–223 take up an old philosophical dispute: is probability just our ignorance, or does nature contain real chance? Knight sides with real indeterminacy and the “principle of cogent reason” (the belief that a die lands unpredictably because there is positively no reason for one face over another, not just that we know no reason). The dispute does not change his later argument. For the thesis you only need the conclusion that statistical probabilities are empirical generalisations over groups and become shakier as the groups become less alike.\n\n2.6 The third type: estimates\nNow Knight’s centrepiece (pp. 224–232). He proposes sorting probability situations by how good a “classification of instances” you can achieve. Three types result."}, {"pdf": 37, "printed": 10, "ch": 2, "text": "Degrees of homogeneity of the 'group of instances' (after Knight, 1921, pp. 224-226)\n\ndice, coins,\ncards\n\nlife\ninsurance\n\nfire\ninsurance\n\nLloyd's coronation cover;\na new factory extension\naccident and\nsickness cover\n\nType 1: a priori\n\nType 2: statistical\n\nType 3: estimate\n\nRISK (measurable)\n\nRISK (measurable)\n\nUNCERTAINTY (not measurable)\n\ninstances fully alike\n\neach instance nearly unique\n\nFigure 2.1. Knight’s three types of probability situation arranged by how alike the grouped instances are. The first two give measurable risk; the third is true uncertainty. The positions of the examples follow Knight’s own examples in Chapters VII and VIII (the order of life, fire and accident insurance is from Chapter VIII, pp. 247–250).\n\nType\n\nWhat it rests on\n\nExample\n\nCan it be insured or\npooled?\n\n1. A priori\n\nAbsolutely homogeneous in- A perfect die; a fair Yes, trivially; no need\nstances differing only in “re- coin.\nto observe anything.\nally indeterminate factors”;\ncalculated from the structure\nof the problem.\n\n2. Statistical\n\nAn empirical count of how of- Mortality rates;\nten X goes with Y; cases not losses.\nperfectly alike.\n\n3. Estimate\n\n“No valid basis of any kind for A manager deciding to No: no group of simiclassifying instances.” expand a factory. lar cases exists to count over.\n\nfire Yes, if the groups are\nalike enough and independence holds.\n\nTable 2.2. Knight’s three-fold classification (pp. 224–225). The quoted phrase is from p. 225.\n\nThe classic example is the factory extension. A manufacturer “considering the advisability of making a large commitment in increasing the capacity of his works” reaches an estimate after weighing the measurable factors (p. 226). What is the probability that his estimate is wrong? “It is manifestly meaningless to speak of either calculating such a probability a priori or of determining it empirically by studying a large number of instances. The essential and outstanding fact is that the ‘instance’ in question is so entirely unique that there are no others” (p. 226). Two features of this passage are easy to miss and often examined. First, the businessman still forms a judgment of confidence. “The business man himself not merely forms the best estimate he can of the outcome of his actions, but he is likely also to estimate the probability that his estimate is correct” (p. 226). Knight insists these are two"}, {"pdf": 38, "printed": 11, "ch": 2, "text": "separate exercises of judgment: “the formation of an estimate and the estimation of its value” (p. 227). So uncertainty is not the absence of a view. It is the absence of an objective basis for the view. The feeling of confidence is real and it governs action, since “the action which follows upon an opinion depends as much upon the amount of confidence in that opinion as it does upon the favorableness of the opinion itself” (p. 227). Second, Knight refuses to call this a probability in the objective sense. It “appears to be meaningless and fatally misleading to speak of the probability, in an objective sense, that a judgment is correct” (p. 231). But because there is “little hope of breaking away from wellestablished linguistic usage, even when vicious,” he agrees to call it a “third type” of probability judgment, “insisting on its differences from the other types rather than its similarity to them” (p. 231).\n\nCan anything be grouped at all?\nKnight is careful not to say that the first two types are cleanly separated from the third. “Nothing in the universe of experience is absolutely unique any more than any two things are absolutely alike” (p. 227), so with a loose enough idea of similarity you can always form classes. He lists two ways of doing so for estimates. You can group the decision with decisions of other people in similar situations (rarely sensible for a factory extension), or you can group a person’s own decisions over many different situations, to estimate how good that person’s judgment is (p. 228). Employers do this constantly: “the capacity for forming correct judgments … is the principal fact which makes a man serviceable in business” (p. 229). Both groupings give at best a rough regularity, never a measured distribution.\n\n2.7 The conclusion of the chapter\nThe summary on pp. 230–232 contains three sentences to know by heart.\n“The liability of opinion or estimate to error must be radically distinguished from probability or chance of either type, for there is no possibility of forming in any way groups of instances of sufficient homogeneity to make possible a quantitative determination of true probability” (p. 231). “An uncertainty which can by any method be reduced to an objective, quantitatively determinate probability, can be reduced to complete certainty by grouping cases” (pp. 231–232). “It is this true uncertainty which by preventing the theoretically perfect outworking of the tendencies of competition gives the characteristic form of ‘enterprise’ to economic organization as a whole and accounts for the peculiar income of the entrepreneur” (p. 232).\n\nThe logic of the chapter is therefore: (1) profit exists because knowledge is imperfect; (2) some imperfect knowledge is neutralised by pooling, because it comes as a measurable distribution; (3) the rest is not neutralised, and this is what makes entrepreneurs, enterprise and pure profit possible."}, {"pdf": 39, "printed": 12, "ch": 2, "text": "2.8 Common misreadings\nFour things Knight did not say\n\n1. “Uncertainty means we know nothing.” He says opinions exist and carry confidence; what is absent is an objective, measurable distribution.\n2. “Uncertainty is just very small probability or fat tails.” A fat-tailed distribution is still a distribution that someone has estimated. Knight’s point is that for unique situations there is none to estimate.\n3. “Risk and uncertainty are two sharply separated boxes.” He treats it as a\nmatter of degree and says so: there are “all gradations from a perfectly homogeneous group of life or fire hazards at one extreme to an absolutely unique exercise of judgment at the other” (pp. 225–226). The sharp labels are a simplification, useful for analysis.\n4. “The words risk and uncertainty appear in this chapter.” They are formally\ndefined at the start of Chapter VIII (p. 233). Chapter VII gives the three types and the contrast between “probability or chance of either type” and “estimates”.\n\n2.9 What it means for the thesis\nThis section is my inference about the thesis, not Knight’s claim.\nClimate transition risk is a candidate for Knight’s third type. There is no “group of instances” of a global transition to net zero by 2050 against which to count outcomes. The NGFS scenario families (Orderly, Disorderly, Hot House World) are not frequencies; they are stories about how policy, technology and physical damage may unfold. The thesis therefore uses scenarios because it cannot use a measured distribution. But not every climate-related exposure is Type 3. Floods and cyclones in a given district have long records. Defaults on loans to a particular industry in a particular year have a history. A sensible use of Knight is to decompose the thesis’s risk into the parts with a statistical basis (district-level hazard frequency; sectoral default experience) and the parts without (the timing and strength of future policy; the pace of technology). The first can be modelled; the second can only be shown through scenarios and sensitivity. It sharpens the answer to a likely viva question. An examiner can fairly ask: if uncertainty is not measurable, why compute CVaR? A defensible answer is that CVaR is not used to measure Knightian uncertainty. It is used as a conditional stress measure: given a scenario that you state openly, how large is the loss in its worst tail? Knight’s framework explains why the scenarios themselves must be stated as assumptions and not as probabilities. My suggestion is to say this explicitly in the methodology chapter. It gives the information argument a foundation. Knight argues in Chapter VIII that uncertainty makes information a commodity (Chapter 3 below). BRSR disclosure, as a source of firm-level climate data, can be framed as an attempt to turn a little of what is Type 3 into Type 2: to give lenders a classification of firms into groups with comparable emissions behaviour. This is a framing, not something Knight says."}, {"pdf": 40, "printed": 13, "ch": 2, "text": "Do you need the original?\nFor concepts: no. This chapter and the teaching notes give you the argument.\nOpen the original in these cases:\n• You plan to quote the three-type classification or the “estimates” passage. Read pp. 224–229 (about five pages) and check the wording against Table 2.2.\n• You plan to quote the conclusion. Read pp. 230–232.\n• You want to engage with the philosophy of probability (real indeterminacy versus ignorance). Read pp. 218–223. This is optional and not needed for the thesis. All page numbers are Knight’s printed page numbers in the 1921 edition.\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ2.1 Why does Knight say that change, taken alone, does not produce profit?\nQ2.2 List Knight’s four sources of error (Table 2.1) without looking.\nQ2.3 What is the difference between an a priori and a statistical probability? Give one example of each.\nQ2.4 What does Knight mean by a “third type” of probability? Why does he say the name is misleading? Explain\nQ2.5 The Philippines soldiers example shows a trap in statistical reasoning. State the trap and explain how it connects to Knight’s paradox about homogeneity.\nQ2.6 Why does Knight say that the formation of an estimate and the estimation of its value are two separate exercises of judgment? What would be lost if they were treated as one?\nQ2.7 Knight says every instance can be grouped with some others if the standard of similarity is loose enough. Why does this not collapse his distinction between risk and uncertainty? Apply\nQ2.8 For each of the following, say whether it is closest to Type 1, Type 2 or Type 3, and say what would change your classification: (a) the chance that a given Indian corporate borrower defaults next year; (b) the chance that India adopts an economy-wide carbon tax by 2030; (c) the chance of a cyclone hitting a given coastal district in a year; (d) the effect of a carbon tax on the profits of a cement producer.\nQ2.9 Take Table 2.1 and, for each of the four sources of error, give one example from public sector bank lending to a carbon-intensive sector. Viva\nQ2.10 “If climate risk is Knightian uncertainty, probabilities are meaningless, so why does your thesis use CVaR?” Give a three-sentence answer that does not abandon either Knight"}, {"pdf": 41, "printed": 14, "ch": 2, "text": "or CVaR.\nQ2.11 A critic says: “Knight’s distinction is outdated, because Bayesian decision theory treats all uncertainty as subjective probability.” Give one way you might respond using only what Knight says in this chapter.\n\nAnswer sketches\nA2.1 Because if changes followed known laws they would be foreseen and built into costs. Profit arises from the divergence between anticipated and actual outcomes, which comes from ignorance of the future, not from change itself.\nA2.2 Failure to perceive the present accurately and completely; failure to infer the future dependably from the present; failure to know the consequences of our own actions accurately; failure to execute actions in exactly the form intended.\nA2.3 A priori: calculated from the structure of the problem (a perfect die, one chance in six). Statistical: obtained by counting experience (the proportion of buildings that burn in a region). The test is whether you could, in principle, compute it without observation.\nA2.4 Estimates: judgments for which there is no valid basis of classifying instances. He says that calling it “probability” is misleading because it lacks the objective, measurable character of the first two, but he keeps the name because it is entrenched, and insists on the differences.\nA2.5 The trap: comparing groups with different make-ups (soldiers are young and healthy; the general population is not). Knight’s paradox: with perfectly homogeneous groups there would be no probability at all, only uniformity, so probability reasoning needs imperfect homogeneity, and thus classification is always imperfect.\nA2.6 They can differ and err independently: you might form a good estimate and be unsure of it, or a poor estimate and feel confident. Action follows confidence as much as the estimate. Treating them as one would hide the fact that confidence is what drives conduct, and that it can be unrelated to accuracy.\nA2.7 Because the groups it forms for estimates are loose, give no measured proportions, and are not a basis for prediction in a single case. They give “some tendency toward regularity” in aggregate and after the fact, not a computable distribution beforehand. Knight treats the difference as one of degree but does not abolish the distinction.\nA2.8 (a) Mostly Type 2 where there is a long portfolio history, but drifting to Type 3 for a new or unusual borrower. (b) Type 3: unique political decision. (c) Type 2: long hazard records, though climate change may shift the frequencies. (d) Type 3 in the aggregate (policy design and timing unknown) but with Type 2 components (observed cost pass-through in past price changes).\nA2.9 Examples: (1) accounts show assets but not a plant’s real emissions exposure; (2) projecting past sector default rates forward while policy changes the environment; (3) a lending restriction pushes borrowers to informal finance and the effect is unknown; (4) a credit-policy circular is applied inconsistently by branches.\nA2.10 CVaR is not used to measure the unmeasurable. It is a stress measure conditional on"}, {"pdf": 42, "printed": 15, "ch": 2, "text": "stated scenarios: given this story, how large is the loss in the worst tail? The scenarios are openly assumptions, which is exactly Knight’s point that for Type 3 situations we have judgments, not distributions.\nA2.11 Knight accepts that people carry subjective probabilities, which is why he calls the third type “probability” too. His point is that such judgments cannot be checked against a group of cases, so they cannot be pooled or insured, and a theory of profit and enterprise needs the difference. A Bayesian reply tells us how to act; it does not make the estimate objective."}, {"pdf": 43, "printed": 16, "ch": 3, "text": "CHAPTER 3\n\nKnight (1921), Chapter VIII: Structures\nand Methods for Meeting Uncertainty\nSource line\nFrank H. Knight, Risk, Uncertainty, and Profit (1921), Chapter VIII, “Structures and Methods for Meeting Uncertainty”, printed pp. 233–263. In your folder this chapter is a scanned image with no text layer; I read it by optical character recognition and checked the quotations below against that text, so a rare mis-read letter is possible. The matching teaching notes are Knight_Ch8_Teaching_Notes.pdf.\n\n3.1 What the chapter does\nChapter VII asked what uncertainty is. Chapter VIII asks what societies do about it. Knight’s answer is a thesis about institutions: insurance, the large firm, the corporation, speculation, hedging, the entrepreneur, the market itself, the information industry, and management are all, in part, devices for dealing with the fact that the future is not known. Where Chapter VII is philosophy, Chapter VIII is a piece of economic sociology.\n\n3.2 Risk and uncertainty are defined\nThe chapter opens with the formal definition, and it is short enough to quote in full: “To preserve the distinction which has been drawn in the last chapter between the measurable uncertainty and an unmeasurable one we may use the term ‘risk’ to designate the former and the term ‘uncertainty’ for the latter” (p. 233).\n\nHe adds the matching terms “objective” and “subjective” probability. And he gives the operational test that you will use most often: “The practical difference between the two categories, risk and uncertainty, is that in the former the distribution of the outcome in a group of instances is known (either through calculation a priori or from statistics of past experience), while in the case of uncertainty this is not true, the reason being in general that it is impossible to form a group of instances, because the situation dealt with is in a high degree unique” (p. 233). The one-question test\n\nCan you form a meaningful group of cases like this one, and know how often each outcome occurs in the group? If yes, it is risk. If you cannot, because the case is too unique, it is uncertainty."}, {"pdf": 44, "printed": 17, "ch": 3, "text": "Knight then does something that is easy to overlook and is very useful. He says that for a person facing a single decision, the two feel the same: “when an individual instance only is at issue, there is no difference for conduct between a measurable risk and an unmeasurable uncertainty” (p. 234). The individual “throws his estimate of the value of an opinion into the probability form of ‘a successes in b trials’ … and ‘feels’ toward it as toward any other probability situation.” The difference appears only when you can group cases, because then risk can be pooled away and uncertainty cannot. He qualifies this in two ways (pp. 234–235): if a true probability can be calculated, it matters not whether one bets once or many times; and some tendency for errors to cancel does exist when one groups a single person’s judgments, though the elements cannot be separated and measured.\n\n3.3 How people feel about uncertainty\nThe next section (pp. 235–237) is a short catalogue of well-observed departures from cool rationality, which the teaching notes rightly call prescient. People will risk a small stake for a large win even when the odds are long, “while they commonly will refuse to incur a small chance of losing a larger amount for a virtual certainty of winning a smaller, even though the actuarial value of the chance is in their favor” (pp. 235–236, citing Adam Smith). They have “an inveterate belief … in his own ‘luck’,” strongest when the uncertainty concerns their own judgment (p. 236). And “superstitions” are nearly universal: “Any coincidence that strikes attention is likely to be elevated into a law of nature” (p. 236). Having noted this, Knight chooses to put it aside and restrict attention to rational conduct. The assumption he keeps is simple and central: “the expected reward must be larger in order to evoke the sacrifice if it is viewed as contingent than if it is considered certain” (p. 236), and the relevant uncertainty is the felt one, “what the man believes the chances to be” (p. 237). Because a person might form an estimate of an objective probability and then also doubt the estimate, “the degree of felt uncertainty is a product of two probability ratios” (p. 237). All economic uncertainty, he concludes, can be treated as “cases of choice between a smaller reward more confidently and a larger one less confidently anticipated” (p. 237).\n\n3.4 Why economic life is saturated with uncertainty\nProduction takes time, and therefore needs two forecasts at once (pp. 237–238). The producer must estimate (a) what the operations will physically yield and (b) what the wants will be that those goods are meant to satisfy. Both are in the future. Knight is also careful about the other side of the matter: “rational conduct strives to reduce to a minimum the uncertainties involved in adapting means to ends. This does not mean … that uncertainty as such is abhorrent to the human species, which probably is not true. We should not really prefer to live in a world where everything was ‘cut and dried”’ (p. 238). We try to reduce uncertainty even though we would not want it eliminated."}, {"pdf": 45, "printed": 18, "ch": 3, "text": "3.5 The methods for meeting uncertainty\nPages 238–240 contain the architecture of the chapter. Uncertainty can be reduced for two basic reasons. First, “uncertainties are less in groups of cases than in single instances”. Second, people differ in how they stand towards it, so it can be given to those best suited to carry it. These give the two fundamental methods, consolidation (grouping) and specialization. Knight then adds others, in order: (3) control of the future, (4) increased power of prediction, a possible fifth, diffusion of the consequences of bad outcomes, and (6) directing activity towards lines in which little uncertainty is involved (pp. 239–240). The sixth is dealt with only briefly at the end (p. 263), where he says he is “very skeptical” that society abhors uncertainty so much as to reduce output below what an equivalent fixed cost would bring.\n\nUncertainty about the future\n(errors of judgment, change)\n\n1. Consolidation\n(grouping,\ninsurance,\nbigger firms)\n\n2. Specialisation\n(entrepreneurs,\nspeculators)\n\n4. Better\nprediction\n(knowledge)\n\n3. Control of\nthe future\n\nmeasurable risk uncertainty moves\nbecomes a cost\nto those best\nable to bear it\n\n5. Diffusion\n(spread\nthe loss)\n\n6. Choose less\nuncertain lines\nof activity\n\n'the objective of all\nrational conduct'\n\nEntrepreneurship, speculation, the corporation and the information industry are structures built mainly from methods 1 and 2 (Knight, 1921, Ch. VIII).\n\nFigure 3.1. Knight’s methods for meeting uncertainty (pp. 238–240), in his order. The two “fundamental” methods are the first two. Is Type 3 uncertainty really irreducible?\n\nThe teaching notes in your folder say that Type 3 “cannot” be reduced by pooling. That is too strong. Knight writes that “even the third type, true uncertainties, show some tendency toward regularity when grouped on the basis of nearly any similarity or common element” (p. 239). His position is more careful: such estimates do tend to fall into groups where errors offset, “but this happens only after the fact and … can only to a limited extent be made the basis of prediction” (p. 251). And an outside agency such as an insurer cannot do the grouping because of moral hazard (next sections). What can work is consolidation inside one organisation that controls the decisions. So uncertainty is not abolished by pooling; it is partly tamed within firms and borne by specialists. For a viva, prefer the wording “cannot be insured by an outsider” to “cannot be pooled at all”."}, {"pdf": 46, "printed": 19, "ch": 3, "text": "Method\n\nHow it works\n\nWhere Knight finds it\n\nConsolidation\n\nMany cases grouped, so errors cancel Insurance; production for a market; the\nand the average becomes steady.\nlarge firm; the corporation; investing in\nmany companies.\n\nSpecialization\n\nUncertainty concentrated in those Free enterprise; speculation; promoters;\nwho are better at, or more willing to renting rather than owning risky assets. bear, it.\n\nControl of the fu- Shape events instead of forecasting Mentioned as part of general progress ture them. (p. 239). Better prediction More knowledge reduces ignorance. Diffusion\n\nInformation services, statistical bureaus,\nconsultants (pp. 260–263).\n\nSpread any loss thinly across many Shareholding in several firms (p. 254).\npeople.\n\nChoice of activity Prefer lines of business with less un- Discussed only briefly (p. 263). certainty. Table 3.1. Methods for meeting uncertainty, with where each appears in Chapter VIII.\n\n3.6 Consolidation 1: the market itself\nThe most interesting application comes first (pp. 240–241). Why do firms produce for a general market and not to the individual order of each consumer? Knight’s answer is that this is itself a response to uncertainty. The consumer does not know in advance what he will want, how much, or how badly. But “the consumer is, to himself, only one; to the producer he is a mere multitude in which individuality is lost. It turns out that an outsider can foresee the wants of a multitude with more ease and accuracy than an individual can attain with respect to his own” (p. 241). The law of large numbers is at work. This is why, he says, we get “the most fundamental feature of the economic system, production for a market.”\n\n3.7 Individuals differ: five variables\nNot everyone stands the same way towards uncertainty, and this is what makes specialization possible. Knight lists five features in which people differ (pp. 241–242). It helps to see them as a sequence from seeing to acting to feeling. He then observes that the first three each have four independent dimensions, namely accuracy, promptness, time range and space range (p. 243), and that learning is a fifth, changing, dimension, since “knowledge is more a matter of learning than of the exercise of absolute judgment” (p. 243). From the standpoint of the person, he says, the five collapse to two: the felt uncertainty and the attitude towards it (p. 242).\n\n3.8 From the handicraft shop to free enterprise\nKnight pictures a pure “handicraft stage” in which each person makes one finished good for a market and consumes many (p. 244). Even that stage is unstable, because it still leaves"}, {"pdf": 47, "printed": 20, "ch": 3, "text": "Variable\n\nMeaning\n\nCapacity to form correct Perceiving and inferring the future correctly. Differs by probjudgments lem: some are good with nature, others at “reading human nature”.\n\nCapacity to judge means\n\nWorking out the steps and adjustments needed.\n\nCapacity to execute\n\nCarrying out plans and adjustments.\n\nDegree of confidence\n\nHow sure one feels, “in large measure independent of the\n‘true value’ of the judgments” (p. 242).\n\nConative attitude\n\nOne’s feeling towards uncertainty itself: some “want to\nbe sure”, others “prefer rather than … shun uncertainty”\n(p. 242).\n\nTable 3.2. Five ways people differ in relation to uncertainty (pp. 241–242). He adds a sixth factor: “occurrences so revolutionary and unexpected by any one as hardly to be brought under the category of an error in judgment at all” (p. 242).\n\neach producer carrying the uncertainty of what to make. The next step, free enterprise, adds specialization. The task of forecasting wants and directing production is taken out of the hands of “the great mass of producers” and placed in charge of “a limited class of ‘entrepreneurs’ or ‘business men’. The bulk of the producing population cease to exercise responsible control over production and take up the subsidiary rôle of furnishing productive resources (labor, land, and capital) to the entrepreneur, placing them under his sole direction for a fixed contract price” (pp. 244–245). In plain terms: workers, landlords and lenders receive fixed payments; the entrepreneur takes whatever is left, good or bad, and bears the uncertainty. Chapter IX of Knight’s book takes this up in detail.\n\n3.9 Insurance and the limits of measurement\nKnight returns to insurance, his standard example of consolidation, and asks how far it can reach (pp. 245–251). Insurance works when you can “assimilate” a situation to “a group of similars and find the proportions” of each outcome (pp. 245–246). The test is accuracy of classification. And there is a further requirement: the insurer must plausibly show that each insured person is paying a fair share according to his own risk (p. 246). Different lines of insurance occupy different positions on the spectrum from measurable to guesswork. Table 3.3 summarises Knight’s tour of them.\n\nWhere insurance stops: the uninsurable business risk\nThen comes the sentence that explains why entrepreneurs exist. “The typical uninsurable (because unmeasurable and this because unclassifiable) business risk relates to the exercise of judgment in the making of decisions by the business man” (p. 251). Two reasons prevent an outside insurer from taking it. First, such estimates tend to offset one another only after the fact, and that is no use for pricing a policy beforehand. Second, there is the “peculiarly obstinate connection of a moral hazard”: “The decisive factors in the case are so largely on the inside of the person making the decisions that the ‘instances’ are not amenable to objective"}, {"pdf": 48, "printed": 21, "ch": 3, "text": "Line\n\nKnight’s assessment\n\nPage\n\nLife insurance\n\nThe most “mathematical” branch. Medical examination plus pp. 247–248\nage, sex, residence, occupation and habits select risks “which\nclosely approximate the ideal of mechanical probability”.\n\nSickness and acci- “An objective description and classification of cases is impos- p. 248 dent sible”; the business is “fraught with great difficulties”; premiums cost “vastly more than they should”. Fire insurance\n\nUnusual\n(Lloyd’s)\n\nOnly recently has it formed “fairly homogeneous classes”. pp. 249–250\nMuch room for judgment even after thousands of classes. Insurance covers only direct loss, never the loss from business interruption, so there is “a large margin of uncertainty”. Fires also spread, so risks are not fully independent.\n\nrisks From “something like the statistical certainty of life insurance pp. 250–251 at one extreme to almost pure guesswork at the other”, for example a royal coronation going ahead as scheduled. Even then “a certain vague grouping of cases on the basis of intuition or judgment” is used.\n\nTable 3.3. Knight’s tour of insurance lines, from most to least measurable.\n\ndescription and external control” (p. 251). If an insurer guaranteed an entrepreneur against the failure of his own judgment, the entrepreneur would have little reason to judge well. Moral hazard in plain words\n\nMoral hazard is the change in behaviour that happens when someone else bears the cost of your mistakes. A fire policy covers a burning building, but the owner still has reason not to light the fire. A policy guaranteeing success of a business plan would remove the very incentive that makes the plan careful. Knight uses the idea to explain why the most important business risks are never insured by outsiders.\n\n3.10 Consolidation 2: bigger firms and the corporation\nIf an outsider cannot do the grouping, the grouping can be done inside one organisation: the same person (or tightly controlled firm) makes many decisions, so that good and bad guesses offset. “In so far as a single business man, by borrowing capital or otherwise, can extend the scope of his exercise of judgment over a greater number of decisions or estimates, there is a greater probability that bad guesses will be offset by good ones and that a degree of constancy and dependability in the total results will be achieved” (p. 252). Knight suggests that this incentive explains, in part, “the phenomenal growth in the average size of industrial establishments”. The chain of organisational forms (single proprietor, partnership, corporation) follows from two needs: to widen the scope of operations, and to “unify interests” so as to reduce moral hazard (pp. 252–254). The corporation has “solved the organization problem” as to scale, since there “appears to be hardly any limit to the magnitude of enterprise” that can be financed by selling shares. On the second need he is sceptical: “nothing but a revolutionary transformation in"}, {"pdf": 49, "printed": 22, "ch": 3, "text": "human nature itself can apparently solve this problem finally” (pp. 253–254).\nHe adds two points that are often missed.\n• Diffusion. The tradability of small shares lets an investor spread holdings over many firms. This gives a second offsetting effect, and also means “the chance of loss of a small fraction of his total resources is of less moment even proportionally than a chance of losing a larger part” (p. 254).\n• Limited liability is not the main gain. “The mere fact of limited liability only serves to transfer losses in excess of invested resources from the owners of the concern to its creditors” (p. 255), so by itself it merely moves risk around. The real reduction of risk comes from the “twofold consolidation” together with greater publicity (p. 255).\n\n3.11 Specialization: speculation, hedging and promotion\nThe second main principle is specialization, with speculation the main example (pp. 255–258). The standard illustration is the hedge. A flour miller who buys wheat now and sells flour later can use a hedging contract “to eliminate the chance of loss or gain due to changes in the value of materials … ‘shifting’ this risk to the professional speculator” (p. 256). Why can the speculator bear it better? Not mainly because of superior judgment, but because of sheer scale: “Where a single flour miller or cotton spinner would be in the market once, the speculator enters it hundreds or thousands of times, and his errors in judgment must show a correspondingly stronger tendency to cancel out” (p. 256). Specialization thus works through consolidation. The same logic separates the founding of new businesses from running them (promotion, p. 257). The promoter expects some ventures to fail and counts “on making his gains from the occasional conspicuous successes”. “He does not ‘expect’ to have his ‘expectations’ verified by the results in every case; the expectations on which he really counts are based on an average, on an ‘estimate’ of the long-run value of his ‘estimates’ ” (pp. 257–258). Insurer versus speculator: Knight’s careful distinction (pp. 258–259)\n\nThe insurer knows more about a particular fire risk, but the real risk is no smaller because he takes it; his risk is smaller only because he takes many. The speculator or entrepreneur who takes over someone’s uncertainty also substitutes his own judgment for the original person’s. If his judgment is better, which is likely for a specialist, the individual risk is genuinely lower and there is “better management, greater economy in the use of economic resources”. So uncertainty-bearing and management cannot be separated: “from the standpoint of the ultimately responsible manager, the two functions are always inseparable” (p. 260).\n\n3.12 Information, advertising and advice\nBecause ignorance is everywhere, knowledge becomes a good that is produced and sold. Knight’s list (pp. 260–263) runs from the specialised services for businessmen, to the vast spending on advertising for consumers, to the experts who “do not stop at diagnosis; in addition they prescribe”. His conclusion: “the ubiquitous presence of uncertainty permeating"}, {"pdf": 50, "printed": 23, "ch": 3, "text": "every relation of life has brought it about that information is one of the principal commodities that the economic organization is engaged in supplying” (p. 261). He is also dry about it: from the economic point of view “it is not material whether the ‘information’ is false or true, or whether it is merely hypnotic suggestion”, since the consumer is the final judge (pp. 261–262). The teaching notes’ three-way split, information, persuasion, guidance, is a fair summary of this passage, though the labels are theirs.\n\n3.13 A figure: why pooling works, and where it fails\n\nStd. deviation of the pool's loss rate\n(percentage points)\n\nKnight’s discussion of consolidation rests on the law of large numbers. Figure 3.2 makes the logic visible and shows what Knight does not dwell on: pooling removes the risk from independent cases, but does not remove the part that comes from a shock common to all the cases. Knight notes the point briefly for fire, where risks “are not entirely independent” because “a fire once started is likely to spread” (p. 250).\n\nloans sharing one common shock\n(correlation 0.15): a floor remains\n\nindependent loans: risk pools\naway, falling as 1/√ n\n\n100\n\n101\n\n102\n\n103\n\n104\n\nNumber of loans in the pool\n\nFigure 3.2. Spread (standard deviation) of the loss rate of a pool of loans, each with a 5% chance of loss. With independent loans, the spread of the pool’s loss rate falls as 1/ n. When the loans share a common shock (here a correlation of 0.15 p betweenpany two loans), a floor remains however many loans are added. The curves use the formula p(1 − p) ρ + (1 − ρ)/n with p = 0.05; ρ = 0 for the lower curve and ρ = 0.15 for the upper. This is my illustration, not a figure from Knight; the numbers are chosen to show the shape.\n\n3.14 What it means for the thesis\nAgain, this section is my inference.\nBanks as consolidators, and the limit of consolidation. A bank is largely a device for consolidation: many loans, many borrowers, errors that offset. Figure 3.2 shows why that logic is weak for climate risk. A transition policy or a physical hazard hits many borrowers in the same sector or region at the same time, so the correlation is high and the floor is high. This"}, {"pdf": 51, "printed": 24, "ch": 3, "text": "is a precise way of stating why portfolio diversification across within-sector borrowers does not protect a bank against transition risk, and it supports your use of sectoral exposure weights rather than treating loans as independent. Moral hazard and public sector banks. Knight’s argument that outsiders cannot insure judgment because of moral hazard has a direct echo in public sector banking, where decisions are made by salaried officers, the bank’s owner is the state, and recapitalisation has been available. That structure changes who ultimately bears the uncertainty. It is part of why the thesis treats PSB ownership as one of its structural differences, and Knight gives you a general theory of why who bears uncertainty matters. How far this applies in India is for your institutional chapters to show; Knight does not speak to it. Specialisation: who is the uncertainty-bearer? Knight says uncertainty flows to specialists. In a transition, one can ask who these specialists are: equity holders of the energy firms, green-bond investors, the government, or the bank’s depositors. Mapping who carries Type 3 climate uncertainty in the Indian financial system is a clean, original question that can be asked in the language of this chapter. Information as the response. If “information is one of the principal commodities” because of uncertainty, then SEBI’s BRSR regime can be read as the state supplying such a commodity. Rating agencies, ESG data vendors and consultants are the private suppliers Knight describes. The thesis’s taxonomy can then be described as a way of classifying firms so that some part of what was unclassifiable becomes statistical. Do you need the original? For concepts: no. The argument above, with the page references, is enough. Open the original only if you intend to quote:\n• the definition of risk and uncertainty and the operational test: p. 233;\n• the statement that for a single instance there is “no difference for conduct”: p. 234;\n• the list of methods and the sentence about Type 3 regularity: pp. 238–240 (this is where the teaching notes are too strong);\n• the uninsurable business risk and moral hazard: p. 251;\n• information as a commodity: p. 261.\nBecause your copy of this chapter is a scan, always check wording against the page image before quoting; my quotations are from OCR.\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ3.1 State Knight’s definitions of risk and uncertainty and the one-question test that separates them.\nQ3.2 List his methods for meeting uncertainty in order. Which two does he call fundamental?\nQ3.3 Name Knight’s five variables on which people differ in relation to uncertainty.\nQ3.4 Why, according to Knight, can an outside insurer not insure a business decision?"}, {"pdf": 52, "printed": 25, "ch": 3, "text": "Explain\nQ3.5 Why does Knight say that for a single decision there is no difference for conduct between risk and uncertainty, and why does the difference nevertheless matter for the economy as a whole?\nQ3.6 Explain how “production for a market” is a response to uncertainty, in Knight’s argument.\nQ3.7 Explain why limited liability is not, for Knight, the main way in which the corporation reduces risk.\nQ3.8 How does Knight distinguish the insurer from the speculator? Why does the distinction lead him to say that uncertainty-bearing and management are inseparable? Apply\nQ3.9 Use Figure 3.2 to explain, in four or five sentences, why a bank cannot diversify away transition risk by lending to more borrowers within the same carbon-intensive sector.\nQ3.10 Place the following on Knight’s spectrum of insurability (Table 3.3) and say why: (a) crop insurance in a monsoon-dependent district; (b) insurance against loss from a future domestic carbon price; (c) a credit-guarantee scheme for small borrowers.\nQ3.11 Who, in the Indian financial system, ends up bearing the part of transition uncertainty that cannot be pooled or insured? Give your answer as a chain of specialisation in Knight’s sense. Viva\nQ3.12 “Knight’s framework says uncertainty can be reduced inside firms by scale. So large Indian banks, with huge loan books, should be well placed to handle climate uncertainty. Why is that wrong?” (Use Figure 3.2.)\nQ3.13 A supervisor says: “If information is a commodity produced because of uncertainty, then mandatory disclosure just produces more noise.” Give an answer that uses Knight’s distinction between information, persuasion and guidance.\n\nAnswer sketches\nA3.1 Risk: measurable uncertainty; the distribution of outcomes in a group of instances is known, by calculation or by past statistics. Uncertainty: unmeasurable; the situation is too unique to form a group. Test: can you form a meaningful group of similar cases and know the frequency of each outcome?\nA3.2 Consolidation, specialization, control of the future, better prediction, (possibly) diffusion, choice of less uncertain activities. The first two are fundamental.\nA3.3 Capacity to form correct judgments; capacity to judge means; capacity to execute; degree of confidence (largely independent of accuracy); conative attitude (feeling towards uncertainty itself).\nA3.4 Because the relevant uncertainty lies in the decision-maker’s own judgment: the insurer cannot classify or control the “instance”, and the insured would have less reason to take"}, {"pdf": 53, "printed": 26, "ch": 3, "text": "care (moral hazard). Estimates also only offset after the fact.\nA3.5 For one decision the person acts on a felt probability whether it is objective or not. The difference matters when you aggregate: measurable risk can be removed by grouping, uncertainty cannot be removed by an outside party. It therefore decides who bears what.\nA3.6 The consumer cannot foresee his own wants, but a producer facing a multitude can foresee their aggregate wants better than each can foresee his own, by the law of large numbers. Producing ahead for the market is therefore a consolidation.\nA3.7 Because limited liability only moves losses beyond invested capital from owners to creditors. The real reduction comes from combining many ventures under unified control (consolidation) plus publicity and diffusion across shareholders.\nA3.8 The insurer’s risk is lower because he takes many cases, not because he knows more about each. The speculator or entrepreneur substitutes his own judgment, which if better lowers the individual risk too, so he is also doing better management. The two roles cannot be separated for the person ultimately responsible.\nA3.9 Loans in the same sector or region face the same policy or physical shock, so their outcomes move together. The pool’s loss spread falls with size only for the independent part; the common-shock part leaves a floor. Adding more borrowers in the same sector adds numbers but not independence.\nA3.10 (a) Partly insurable: long district-level weather history gives statistical probabilities, but with correlated losses across the district. (b) Closer to the uninsurable end: unique policy event, no group of cases, and moral hazard (firms can lobby or time investment). (c) Insurable to the extent that default history for small borrowers allows classification, though losses correlate in downturns.\nA3.11 A defensible chain: lenders pass uncertainty to the banks; the banks bear it as claims on borrowers; public sector owners and the state bear it through recapitalisation; ultimately taxpayers. Compare with private actors (equity holders, green investors) who specialise voluntarily. The answer is yours to defend; the point is the ordering and what each party can pool.\nA3.12 Because a bigger loan book consolidates independent errors, not shared ones. Transition and physical shocks correlate across borrowers in a sector or region, so size alone leaves a floor of risk. Scale also does not help with the unknowable timing of policy.\nA3.13 Knight separates information that reduces ignorance, persuasion that merely moves belief, and guidance that prescribes. Disclosure aimed at reducing ignorance (standardised metrics that allow classification) is information; the risk is that disclosure turns into persuasion (marketing). So the design of disclosure matters, not just its volume."}, {"pdf": 54, "printed": 27, "ch": 4, "text": "CHAPTER 4\n\nKeynes (1937): “The General Theory of\nEmployment”\nSource line\nJ. M. Keynes, “The General Theory of Employment”, Quarterly Journal of Economics 51(2), February 1937, pp. 209–223. In your reading plan this was listed as a summary of Chapter 12 of the General Theory. It is not. It is a fifteen-page reply to critics (Taussig, Leontief, Robertson and Viner, whose comments had appeared in the November 1936 issue) in which Keynes restates his core ideas. It is, however, the single best short statement of his theory of uncertainty. Page references below are to the printed journal pages. The text layer of your PDF is clean.\n\n4.1 Why this short paper matters\nThe General Theory (1936) is long and sometimes loose. In 1937 Keynes was pressed by critics and responded by stating, in his own words, what he thought was essential. He says: “I am more attached to the comparatively simple fundamental ideas which underlie my theory than to the particular forms in which I have embodied them” (p. 211). What he then writes is a compact argument with three parts, and the whole of what economists later call “Keynesian uncertainty” is in it. The argument in four sentences\n\nWe cannot calculate the future for the things that matter most to wealth. We cope by leaning on conventions, which are flimsy and liable to sudden collapse. Holding money is a barometer of how much we distrust those conventions, so the interest rate is the price of that distrust. Because investment rides on these shaky foundations, and because income depends on investment through the multiplier, output and employment fluctuate.\n\n4.2 Section I: the replies (pp. 209–212)\nThe first section answers the four critics. You do not need the detail, but two points are worth knowing because they recur.\n• The homogeneity postulate (Leontief, p. 209). Keynes agrees with Leontief that he differs from orthodoxy on this, adding that experience contradicts the postulate and that “it is for those who make a highly special assumption to justify it”. He says it enters mainly through the way the quantity of money determines the interest rate."}, {"pdf": 55, "printed": 28, "ch": 4, "text": "• Hoarding and liquidity preference (Viner, pp. 210–211). Viner asked for statistics showing that liquidity preference matters, since most people invest their savings at the best rate. Keynes replies that the relevant person is the marginal potential hoarder. In a crisis, liquidity preference rises, and “this shows itself not so much in increased hoards … as in a sharp rise in the rate of interest, i.e. securities fall in price”. A rise in the interest rate is “a means alternative to an increase of hoards for satisfying an increased liquidity-preference” (p. 211). The second point is subtle and worth remembering: liquidity preference does not need to show up as piles of cash. It shows up as a price, the interest rate and with it the prices of assets.\n\n4.3 Section II: uncertain knowledge and its consequences (pp. 212–\n219)\nThis is the heart of the paper.\n\n4.3.1 What the classical economists assumed\nKeynes begins with a charge. Ricardo, Marshall and their successors worked with systems in which “the amount of the factors employed was given and the other relevant facts were known more or less for certain” (p. 212). This did not rule out change or even disappointed expectations. But “risks, of which, tho admitted, not much notice was taken, were supposed to be capable of an exact actuarial computation. The calculus of probability … was supposed to be capable of reducing uncertainty to the same calculable status as that of certainty itself” (p. 213). Why does this matter more for economics than for other fields? Because of the nature of wealth: “The whole object of the accumulation of Wealth is to produce results, or potential results, at a comparatively distant, and sometimes at an indefinitely distant, date. Thus the fact that our knowledge of the future is fluctuating, vague and uncertain, renders Wealth a peculiarly unsuitable subject for the methods of the classical economic theory” (p. 213). A world in which goods are consumed soon after being produced would suit the classical method; a world that accumulates wealth for an indefinite future does not.\n\n4.3.2 What “uncertain” means\nThis passage is the one most often cited, so read it carefully (pp. 213–214).\n“By ‘uncertain’ knowledge, let me explain, I do not mean merely to distinguish what is known for certain from what is only probable. The game of roulette is not subject, in this sense, to uncertainty; nor is the prospect of a Victory bond being drawn. Or, again, the expectation of life is only slightly uncertain. Even the weather is only moderately uncertain. The sense in which I am using the term is that in which the prospect of a European war is uncertain, or the price of copper and the rate of interest twenty years hence, or the obsolescence of a new invention, or the position of private wealth-owners in the social system in 1970. About these matters there is no scientific basis on which to form any calculable probability whatever. We simply do not know.”"}, {"pdf": 56, "printed": 29, "ch": 4, "text": "Notice the shape of the argument. Keynes uses a scale: roulette, bond lotteries, life expectancy and the weather lie on the calculable side. War, long-run commodity prices and interest rates, technological obsolescence and the social order lie on the other. This is the same distinction as Knight’s, reached independently (Keynes’s Treatise on Probability and Knight’s book both appeared in 1921). The two lists even use the same kind of examples. Where they differ is in what they do with the idea. Knight uses it to explain profit. Keynes uses it to explain money, interest and investment. Knight (1921)\n\nKeynes (1937)\n\nName for the cal- Risk (measurable)\nculable\n\n“Probable” (roulette, life expectancy,\nweather)\n\nName\nother\n\n“Uncertain” knowledge\n\nfor\n\nthe Uncertainty (unmeasurable)\n\nCriterion\n\nNo valid basis for grouping instances.\n\n“No scientific basis on which to form any\ncalculable probability.”\n\nTypical examples\n\nA new business venture; a unique deci- European war; copper price and intersion. est rate twenty years on; obsolescence; wealth-owners’ position in 1970.\n\nWhat it explains\n\nProfit; the entrepreneur; the firm.\n\nHow people cope\n\nInsurance, specialisation, the firm, infor- Three techniques / conventions.\nmation.\n\nLiquidity preference; interest; unstable\ninvestment; fluctuating employment.\n\nTable 4.1. Knight and Keynes on uncertainty compared. The core distinction is the same; the use made of it differs.\n\n4.3.3 The three techniques\nHaving said “we simply do not know”, Keynes asks how we nevertheless act. “The necessity for action and for decision compels us as practical men to do our best to overlook this awkward fact and to behave exactly as we should if we had behind us a good Benthamite calculation” (p. 214). He names three techniques (p. 214): (1) The present is a guide to the future. “We assume that the present is a much more serviceable guide to the future than a candid examination of past experience would show it to have been hitherto.” (2) Prices embody correct opinion. “We assume that the existing state of opinion as expressed in prices and the character of existing output is based on a correct summing up of future prospects.” (3) Follow the crowd. “Knowing that our own individual judgment is worthless, we endeavor to fall back on the judgment of the rest of the world which is perhaps better informed. … The psychology of a society of individuals each of whom is endeavoring to copy the others leads to what we may strictly term a conventional judgment.”"}, {"pdf": 57, "printed": 30, "ch": 4, "text": "Reading the three techniques in plain words\n\n(1) Assume things will continue as they are. (2) Assume the market price already knows what we do not. (3) When unsure, do what everyone else does. Each is sensible for one person. Together they make a system in which everyone is relying on everyone else, and nobody has an independent foundation. Keynes draws the consequence at once: “being based on so flimsy a foundation, it is subject to sudden and violent changes. The practice of calmness and immobility, of certainty and security, suddenly breaks down. New fears and hopes will, without warning, take charge of human conduct. The forces of disillusion may suddenly impose a new conventional basis of valuation” (pp. 214–215). And he adds the memorable line: “All these pretty, polite techniques, made for a well-panelled Board Room and a nicely regulated market, are liable to collapse. At all times the vague panic fears and equally vague and unreasoned hopes are not really lulled, and lie but a little way below the surface” (p. 215). He turns this on economics itself: classical theory is “one of these pretty, polite techniques which tries to deal with the present by abstracting from the fact that we know very little about the future” (p. 215).\n\n4.3.4 Why anyone holds money: the interest rate as a barometer of distrust\nHere the argument moves from epistemology to monetary theory. Money has two functions, a money of account and a store of wealth. As a store of wealth it is “barren” while almost every other form of wealth yields interest or profit. “Why should anyone outside a lunatic asylum wish to use money as a store of wealth?” (pp. 215–216). His answer is the most quoted passage of the paper: “our desire to hold Money as a store of wealth is a barometer of the degree of our distrust of our own calculations and conventions concerning the future. … The possession of actual money lulls our disquietude; and the premium which we require to make us part with money is the measure of the degree of our disquietude” (p. 216). The consequence for the theory of interest follows. The orthodox view looked at the quantity of money hoarded. Keynes says that what changes with confidence is “not the amount that is actually hoarded, but the amount of the premium which has to be offered to induce people not to hoard.” So changes in liquidity preference “primarily affect, not prices, but the rate of interest” (p. 216). The rate of interest is, in his words, “the premium which has to be offered to induce people to hold their wealth in some form other than hoarded money” (p. 216), and it is “the factor which adjusts at the margin the demand for hoards to the supply of hoards” (p. 217).\n\n4.3.5 Three stages from interest to investment\nKeynes then sets out a chain with stages. The plain version is below, and Figure 4.1 shows it.\n1. Stage 1: the interest rate. It is fixed by the quantity of money and the propensity to hoard (liquidity preference), as above (pp. 216–217)."}, {"pdf": 58, "printed": 31, "ch": 4, "text": "2. Stage 2: the prices of capital assets. Wealth-owners choose between lending at the going rate and buying capital assets. In equilibrium the two must offer equal advantage to the marginal investor. Asset prices adjust until, “having regard to their prospective yields and account being taken of all those elements of doubt and uncertainty, interested and disinterested advice, fashion, convention and what else you will”, they do (p. 217). Opinions about prospective yield also fluctuate sharply, “precisely for the reason already given, namely, the flimsiness of the basis of knowledge on which they depend” (p. 217).\n3. Stage 3: investment. Capital assets can be newly produced; how much is produced depends on the relation between their costs and their expected market prices. High asset prices raise investment; low prices reduce it (pp. 217–218).\n\nKeynes (1937), pp. 213-221: the chain of argument\n\nUnknowable Conventions\nfuture\n(3 techniques)\n\nLiquidity\npreference\n\nRate of\ninterest\n\nPrices of\ncapital assets\n\nInvestment\n\nIncome and\nemployment\n(multiplier)\n\nWeak, shifting foundations at the left are transmitted to output and jobs at the right. The second foundation, opinions about future yields, feeds in at the 'prices of capital assets' step.\n\nFigure 4.1. Keynes’s chain of argument in the 1937 paper (pp. 213–221). Shaky foundations on the left are transmitted to output and employment on the right. (A schematic of my own, built from the text.)\n\n4.3.6 Why investment fluctuates, and why nothing offsets it\n“It is not surprising that the volume of investment, thus determined, should fluctuate widely from time to time. For it depends on two sets of judgments about the future, neither of which rests on an adequate or secure foundation – on the propensity to hoard and on opinions of the future yield of capital-assets” (p. 218). And then the key step: the two sources of instability do not cancel. “Nor is there any reason to suppose that the fluctuations in one of these factors will tend to offset the fluctuations in the other. … Indeed, the conditions which aggravate the one factor tend, as a rule, to aggravate the other. For the same circumstances which lead to pessimistic views about future yields are apt to increase the propensity to hoard” (p. 218). This is a correlation argument\n\nTwo shaky inputs would be tolerable if their errors offset, as in Knight’s grouping (Chapter 3). Keynes says they do the opposite: pessimism about returns and the urge to hold money rise together. In the language of the primer in Chapter 1, the two uncertainties are positively correlated, so pooling them does not reduce risk. Compare Figure 3.2."}, {"pdf": 59, "printed": 32, "ch": 4, "text": "The only self-correcting force comes later and “in an uncertain degree”. Falling investment reduces output, which reduces the need for transaction money, which releases cash for hoards, which lets the interest rate fall, which raises asset prices and investment (p. 218). This is slow, and Keynes expects it to be incomplete. He then notes what would hold if the future were calculable: the liquidity-preference curve would be stable and very inelastic, a small fall in income would cut the interest rate sharply, and “the whole of the available resources would normally be employed; and the conditions required by the orthodox theory would be satisfied” (p. 219). In other words, the orthodox full-employment result needs the assumption of a calculable future. This is the logical point that links his uncertainty to unemployment.\n\n4.4 Section III: demand and supply for output as a whole (pp. 219–\n222)\nThe third section is the theory of effective demand and the multiplier. It matters for your coursework because uncertainty works through it.\n\n4.4.1 Effective demand and the psychological law\nTraditional theory, Keynes says, never worked out a theory of the demand and supply of output as a whole: “the theory of effective demand … having been entirely neglected for more than a hundred years” (p. 219). His own answer is that effective demand has two parts, investment and consumption, and that consumption depends mainly on income. The “psychological law”: when aggregate income rises, consumption rises but by less. “This psychological law was of the utmost importance in the development of my own thought” (p. 220).\n\n4.4.2 The multiplier by arithmetic\nKeynes gives the argument with numbers, and the numbers are worth following. Suppose the public spends nine-tenths of its income on consumption goods. If entrepreneurs produced consumption goods at more than nine times the cost of the investment goods they produce, some of the output could not be sold at cost, because total income is made up of investment income plus consumption income and only nine-tenths of it is spent. So “entrepreneurs will make a loss until they contract their output of consumption-goods down to an amount at which it no longer exceeds nine times their current output of investment goods” (p. 220). The relation is what he calls the Multiplier (p. 220). In symbols, with marginal propensity to consume c and investment I, income is Y = I/(1 − c), so a rise in I raises Y by 1/(1 − c) times as much. With c = 0.9 the multiplier is 10; with c = 0.75 it is 4; with c = 0.5 it is 2. Figure 4.2 shows how successive rounds of spending add up to this limit."}, {"pdf": 60, "printed": 33, "ch": 4, "text": "Cumulative rise in income\n(from a first injection of investment = 100)\n\nChapter 4. Keynes (1937): “The General Theory of Employment”\n\nMPC = 0.9 (Keynes's 'nine-tenths')\nlimit 1000\n\n800\n700\n600\n500\n\nMPC = 0.75\nlimit 400\n\n400\n300\n\nMPC = 0.5\nlimit 200\n\n200\n100\n0.0\n\n2.5\n\n5.0\n\n7.5\n\n10.0\n12.5\nRounds of spending\n\n15.0\n\n17.5\n\n20.0\n\nFigure 4.2. The multiplier as a sum of rounds. An initial injection of 100 of investment is spent in part, re-spent in part, and so on; the cumulative rise approaches 100/(1 − c). The higher the propensity to consume, the larger and slower the build-up. (Illustration using the formula implied by Keynes’s “nine-tenths” example, p. 220; the algebra is the standard one, not written out in the paper.)\n\n4.4.3 What the theory does and does not claim\n“The theory can be summed up by saying that, given the psychology of the public, the level of output and employment as a whole depends on the amount of investment.” He puts investment first “because it is usual in a complex system to regard as the causa causans that factor which is most prone to sudden and wide fluctuation” (p. 221). He lists other factors too (propensity to hoard, monetary policy, confidence about yield, propensity to spend, money-wage factors) and says the unreliable ones are “those which determine the rate of investment, since it is they which are influenced by our views of the future about which we know so little” (p. 221). He is also clear about the limits: “This that I offer is, therefore, a theory of why output and employment are so liable to fluctuation. It does not offer a ready-made remedy” (p. 221). The diagnosis is meant to be “definitive”; the cures are “not worked out completely” and “related to the particular conditions of the time” (pp. 221–222).\n\n4.5 The two summary grounds of departure (pp. 222–223)\nKeynes finishes by listing his main grounds of departure.\n(1) The orthodox theory assumes a kind of knowledge of the future we do not\nhave. The hypothesis of a calculable future “leads to a wrong interpretation of the principles of behavior which the need for action compels us to adopt, and to an underestimation of the concealed factors of utter doubt, precariousness, hope and fear” (p. 222). The result is a mistaken theory of interest. He argues that the marginal efficiency of capital depends on asset prices, which determine investment, which is consistent in equilibrium with only one level of money-income. So “the marginal efficiency of capital is not determined, unless the level of money-income is given” and the orthodox system “is one equation short” unless income is assumed given at full employment (p. 222). His conclusion is that, instead of the marginal efficiency of capital determining the rate of interest, it is closer to the truth (though not"}, {"pdf": 61, "printed": 34, "ch": 4, "text": "the whole truth) to say that the rate of interest determines the marginal efficiency of capital (pp. 222–223). The words at the page break are damaged in the text layer of your PDF, so this is my paraphrase; read the page image before quoting. (2) The orthodox theory has ignored the need for a theory of the supply and demand of output as a whole. Keynes doubts whether many modern economists truly accept Say’s Law (supply creates its own demand), but says they have not noticed they were tacitly assuming it, so “the psychological law underlying the Multiplier has escaped notice” (p. 223).\n\n4.6 Common misreadings\nFour traps\n\n1. “Keynes says markets are irrational.” No: he says that rational people facing unknowable futures adopt conventions, and that the resulting system is fragile.\n2. “Uncertainty means probabilities are subjective.” Not quite. Keynes’s category is knowledge for which no probability can be formed, which is stronger than “subjective probability”.\n3. “Liquidity preference is about hoards of cash.” He explicitly denies this: it works through the price (the interest rate), not the quantity of cash hoarded (pp. 210–211, 216).\n4. “This paper summarises Chapter 12.” It does not; it is a reply to critics. Chapter 12 of the General Theory (“The State of Long-Term Expectation”) is where the convention and “animal spirits” discussions are developed at length. If you want those, you must read it.\n\n4.7 What it means for the thesis\nThis section is my inference; use or discard it.\nConventions and carbon-risk pricing. Keynes’s three techniques map neatly onto how banks and markets price transition risk. Technique 1 is extrapolating today’s policy into the future; technique 2 is trusting today’s asset prices (including carbon-intensive asset prices) as a correct summary of prospects; technique 3 is herding on ratings and peer practice. A disillusion that imposes a “new conventional basis of valuation” is a good description of the repricing that supervisors worry about when climate policy changes abruptly. Why climate stress tests are scenario-based. If transition outcomes were calculable, banks could compute a risk premium. Keynes says that for matters like “the rate of interest twenty years hence” there is no such basis. This is a clean argument for why supervisors and the thesis use scenarios (what if the carbon price follows path A or B) and not a single forecast. Correlated judgments. Keynes’s point that the two shaky judgments reinforce each other in bad times is a point about procyclicality in banks’ capital adequacy. When pessimism about yields rises, liquidity preference rises too. Applied to PSBs: a bad shock reduces asset values"}, {"pdf": 62, "printed": 35, "ch": 4, "text": "and increases the desire to hold liquid assets at once, which tightens credit exactly when capital is needed. How strongly this holds in India is an empirical question. A caution. Using Keynes places the thesis in the Post-Keynesian tradition, and the memory of your own work records that mixing Post-Keynesian ideas with neoclassical econometric tools is a vulnerability an examiner may press. Chapter 6 gives a short note on how to frame this. Do you need the original? For concepts: no. This chapter contains everything the paper argues. Open the original if you plan to quote:\n• the definition of uncertain knowledge: pp. 213–214 (copy the roulette–war passage exactly);\n• the three techniques: p. 214;\n• the “barometer of distrust” passage: p. 216;\n• the argument that the two instabilities do not offset: p. 218;\n• the final two summary grounds, especially the sentence across the pp. 222–223 page break, whose text is damaged in the file. For “animal spirits” and the state of long-term expectation: you need the General Theory, Chapter 12; this paper does not contain them.\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ4.1 What is the meaning of “uncertain” in Keynes’s 1937 paper? Give his examples on both sides of the line.\nQ4.2 State the three techniques by which people cope with uncertainty.\nQ4.3 What, according to Keynes, does the rate of interest measure?\nQ4.4 What is the “psychological law” behind the multiplier?\nExplain\nQ4.5 Why does Keynes say that the quantity of money hoarded is the wrong thing to look at when studying liquidity preference?\nQ4.6 Explain, in words, why the two sources of instability in investment do not offset each other.\nQ4.7 Explain how the multiplier follows from the propensity to consume. Compute the multiplier for c = 0.8.\nQ4.8 In what sense is classical theory “one of these pretty, polite techniques”? Apply\nQ4.9 Choose a recent change in climate policy or disclosure that affected bank-loan or equity valuations in India. Describe it using Keynes’s three techniques and the idea of a “new"}, {"pdf": 63, "printed": 36, "ch": 4, "text": "conventional basis of valuation”.\nQ4.10 A bank sets its capital buffer using a single central forecast of the carbon price. Criticise this using Keynes’s examples of uncertain matters.\nQ4.11 Suppose banks’ liquidity preference rises sharply after a shock. Following Keynes, what happens to the rate of interest, asset prices and investment, and what would a central bank have to do to offset it? Viva\nQ4.12 “Knight and Keynes say the same thing, so citing both is redundant.” Respond using Table 4.1.\nQ4.13 An examiner says: “Keynesian uncertainty means you cannot model anything, yet your thesis is an econometric model.” How do you respond?\n\nAnswer sketches\nA4.1 Not merely “probable” as against “certain”: uncertain means no scientific basis for any calculable probability. Calculable side: roulette, Victory bond, life expectancy, the weather. Uncertain side: a European war, copper price and interest rate twenty years on, obsolescence of an invention, the position of wealth-owners in 1970.\nA4.2 Assume the present is a good guide to the future; assume current prices embody correct opinion; conform to the majority (convention).\nA4.3 The premium needed to induce people to hold wealth in some form other than hoarded money; it is a barometer of distrust of our own calculations and conventions.\nA4.4 When income rises, consumption rises but by less, so the increase is divided between spending and saving.\nA4.5 Because a rise in liquidity preference shows itself mainly as a higher interest rate (a fall in security prices), not as larger hoards; the supply of hoardable cash is limited. The premium is what matters.\nA4.6 The same circumstances that make people pessimistic about future yields also raise the urge to hold money, so the two reinforce each other. The only offsetting force (lower activity releasing cash) arrives later and uncertainly.\nA4.7 If c is the share of income consumed, income is investment divided by (1 − c), so a rise in investment multiplies. For c = 0.8, 1/(1 − 0.8) = 5.\nA4.8 It handles the present by abstracting from the fact that we know little about the future, just as ordinary behaviour does by conventions; it mirrors the market-place’s techniques instead of examining them.\nA4.9 Open answer. A good one identifies who extrapolated the old policy (1), who relied on prices (2), who followed peers (3), and what triggered revaluation.\nA4.10 A single forecast assumes a calculable probability for something Keynes puts on the uncertain side (the interest rate twenty years ahead, obsolescence of technology). Scenarios or ranges are the honest response."}, {"pdf": 64, "printed": 37, "ch": 4, "text": "A4.11 The interest rate rises and asset prices fall, so investment falls. The central bank would have to supply liquidity (increase the quantity of money available) so the premium needed falls.\nA4.12 The core distinction is shared, but Knight is explaining profit and the firm and proposes institutions that tame uncertainty; Keynes is explaining liquidity preference, interest and unstable investment and proposes that conventions are fragile. Different downstream use justifies citing both.\nA4.13 Keynes does not say nothing can be modelled; he says that predictions of the long-run future are not calculable, and that models should be treated as conventions with limited reliability. The thesis can use econometrics to estimate historical relationships and scenarios for conditional what-if analysis while being explicit that it does not forecast the unknowable."}, {"pdf": 65, "printed": 38, "ch": 5, "text": "CHAPTER 5\n\nMinsky (1992): “The Financial Instability Hypothesis”\nSource line and status of this chapter\nHyman P. Minsky, “The Financial Instability Hypothesis”, Levy Economics Institute Working Paper No. 74, May 1992 (prepared for the Handbook of Radical Political Economy, Elgar, 1993). About eight printed pages; the page numbers below are the working paper’s own (1–8). This chapter is self-contained and removable. Chapter 1 explains why the place of Minsky in the thesis is under reconsideration. If you drop Minsky, skip this chapter and Section 6.3. Nothing else depends on it.\n\n5.1 What the paper is\nIt is a compact statement of Minsky’s own theory, written late in his career. Unlike Knight and Keynes, it is not about knowledge; it is about debt. Its claim is that a capitalist economy with expensive capital assets and a sophisticated financial system is prone to endogenous instability: the system itself moves from a robust to a fragile financial structure during good times, without needing an outside shock.\n\n5.2 The argument, step by step\n5.2.1 Empirical starting point and the claim about Keynes (pp. 1–2)\nMinsky begins with the observation that “from time to time, capitalist economies exhibit inflations and debt deflations which seem to have the potential to spin out of control”, in which “the economic system’s reactions to a movement of the economy amplify the movement” (p. 1). This contradicts the view that the economy is “constantly an equilibrium seeking and sustaining system”. He cites Fisher (1933) on debt deflation and Kindleberger (1978) on self-sustaining disequilibrating processes. Then comes his interpretive claim: “As economic theory, the financial instability hypothesis is an interpretation of the substance of Keynes’s ‘General Theory’ ” (p. 1). Note that this is Minsky’s reading of Keynes; other Keynesians dispute it. He also draws on Schumpeter’s credit view of money (p. 2).\n\n5.2.2 The economy as dated promises (pp. 2–3)\nThe economic problem is, following Keynes, “the capital development of the economy” and not “the allocation of given resources among alternative employments” (p. 2). Investment is"}, {"pdf": 66, "printed": 39, "ch": 5, "text": "financed by exchanging present money for future money: present money buys resources for investment; future money is the profits the assets are expected to earn. Control over capital assets is financed by liabilities, “commitments to pay money at dates specified or as conditions arise”. So for each unit, “the liabilities on its balance sheet determine a time series of prior payment commitments, even as the assets generate a time series of conjectured cash receipts” (pp. 2–3). Minsky quotes Keynes’s “veil of money” passage (p. 3) and stresses that it differs from the quantity-theory veil: here money connects economic units through time by financing contracts, with banks at the centre. In the key negotiations “businessmen interpret the numbers and the expectations as enthusiasts, bankers as skeptics” (p. 4). The idea in one line\n\nEvery borrower has a schedule of promised payments (from liabilities) and a schedule of hoped-for receipts (from assets). Stability depends on whether the receipts turn out to be enough for the promises.\n\n5.2.3 What determines whether promises are kept: profits (pp. 5–6)\nMinsky adopts the Kalecki–Levy view in which aggregate demand determines profits. In the “skeletal model” aggregate profits equal aggregate investment in each period; in a richer version, “aggregate profits equal aggregate investment plus the government deficit” (pp. 5–6). The consequence: “Expectations of profits depend upon investment in the future, and realized profits are determined by investment: thus, whether or not liabilities are validated depends upon investment. Investment takes place now because businessmen and their bankers expect investment to take place in the future” (p. 6). Debt is validated only if investment continues. That is why the system can be fragile: investment continues because people expect it to. He also says banks are profit-seeking “merchants of debt” who innovate in the assets they hold and the liabilities they sell, which is why there is no fixed “money” with a constant velocity (p. 6).\n\n5.2.4 Three financing postures (p. 7)\nA likely typo, and a worked example\n\nOn p. 7 the paper says “Governments with floating debts, corporations with floating issues of commercial paper, and banks are typically hedge units.” By his own definitions, units that must roll over debt are speculative, so “hedge” here is almost certainly a slip for “speculative”. Say so only if you are sure of the original; check the page image. Numbers. A firm borrows Rs 100 at 10% for one year. Operating cash is Rs 130: it pays interest (10) and principal (100) with 20 to spare, so it is hedge. If operating cash were Rs 40, it could pay interest (10) but not principal (100): speculative, it must roll over Rs 100. If it were Rs 6, it could not even pay interest: Ponzi, it must borrow or sell assets."}, {"pdf": 67, "printed": 40, "ch": 5, "text": "Posture\n\nMinsky’s definition (p. 7)\n\nWhat it means\n\nHedge\n\nUnits that “can fulfill all of their con- Operating cash covers interest and princitractual payment obligations by their cash pal. Robust. flows”; more equity in the liability structure makes this likelier.\n\nSpeculative\n\nUnits that “can meet their payment com- Cash covers interest but not principal. Vulmitments on ‘income account’ … even as nerable to a refinancing squeeze or a rate they cannot repay the principle out of in- rise. come cash flows”; they must “roll over” debt.\n\nPonzi\n\nCash flows from operations “not sufficient Cash does not cover even interest; borto fulfill either the repayment of principle rowing to pay interest raises liabilities and or the interest due”; units sell assets or bor- “lowers the margin of safety”. row.\n\nTable 5.1. Minsky’s three financing postures. Based on p. 7 of the working paper. Note: “Ponzi” is not about fraud; it is the label for a particular cash-flow position.\n\nCash flow in one period (index)\n\nHedge\n\nSpeculative\n\nPonzi\n\n120\n100\n\nprincipal\nprincipal\n\nprincipal\n\ninterest\n\ninterest\n\ninterest\n\nExpected\ncash income\n\nPayment\ncommitments\n\nExpected\ncash income\n\nPayment\ncommitments\n\nExpected\ncash income\n\nPayment\ncommitments\n\nIllustrative numbers, chosen only to show the three relations of income to debt commitments (after Minsky, 1992, p. 7).\n\nFigure 5.1. The three postures for a unit with the same debt, set against its expected cash income. A schematic built from Minsky’s definitions (p. 7); the numbers are illustrative.\n\n5.2.5 The two theorems and the transition (pp. 7–8)\n“The first theorem of the financial instability hypothesis is that the economy has financing regimes under which it is stable, and financing regimes in which it is unstable.” If hedge finance dominates, the economy “may well be an equilibrium seeking and containing system”; the greater the weight of speculative and Ponzi finance, the more likely it is “a deviation amplifying system” (pp. 7–8). “The second theorem … is that over periods of prolonged prosperity, the economy transits from financial relations that make for a stable system to financial relations that make for an unstable system” (p. 8). The paper adds the rough mechanism: in good times, firms and lenders take on more debt; then, if an economy with many speculative units is inflationary and authorities tighten, “speculative units will become Ponzi units and the net worth of previously Ponzi units will quickly evaporate”. Units short of cash are forced “to try to make position by selling out"}, {"pdf": 68, "printed": 41, "ch": 5, "text": "position”, which is “likely to lead to a collapse of asset values” (p. 8).\nThe paper closes by saying the hypothesis “does not rely upon exogenous shocks to generate business cycles”: cycles come from internal dynamics together with the interventions meant to contain them (p. 8). He also remarks that the larger role of governments as refinancing agents means downside vulnerability of profits has diminished, but may bring an upside inflationary bias (p. 5). The cycle in a sentence each Good times → confidence rises → more borrowing, thinner safety margins → share of speculative and Ponzi units rises → a trigger (tight money, a profit disappointment) → forced asset sales → asset prices fall → net worth collapses. Figure 5.2. The Minsky sequence, from the last page of the paper (p. 8). A simplification of mine.\n\n5.3 What is distinctive, and what is contested\nMinsky’s claim\nEndogenous\nbility\n\nCommon objection\n\ninsta- Stability breeds fragility without out- Hard to test; the trigger often looks exside shocks. ogenous.\n\nReading of Keynes\n\nThe General Theory is a theory of finan- Other Keynesians see it as a theory of cial fragility. effective demand with money playing a different role.\n\nMechanism of uncer- Expectations of profit drive finance and The 1992 paper says little about why tainty validation. expectations are what they are; that is Keynes’s contribution (Chapter 4). Measurability\n\nThree postures can be classified from Needs firm-level cash-flow and debt\ncash flows.\ndata; classification thresholds are not in\nthe paper.\n\nTable 5.2. Where Minsky is distinctive and where it is disputed.\n\n5.4 What it could mean for the thesis, and what dropping it would\ncost\nThis is my analysis of the trade-off you are weighing; the decision is yours.\nDo you need the original?\nFor concepts: no. The working paper is short and everything it argues is here. Open it only to quote: the definitions of the three postures (p. 7), the two theorems (pp. 7–8), the profit-equals-investment statement (pp. 5–6). Check the “hedge” sentence on p. 7 before citing it. For a fuller treatment (the lender’s risk, the financial-fragility model with numbers, policy), you need Minsky’s Stabilizing an Unstable Economy (1986); it is not in your folder and I have not read it."}, {"pdf": 69, "printed": 42, "ch": 5, "text": "Keep a lean Minsky\nWhat stays\n\nDrop Minsky\n\nOne short section: a transition shock Knight and Keynes carry the uncertainty\nturns hedge borrowers into speculative argument; no cycle theory.\nor Ponzi ones; interest-coverage measures\nclassify them.\n\nWhat it gives the A ready bridge from firm-level debt- A tighter theory chapter with fewer thesis servicing capacity to bank capital ad- strands for an examiner to challenge. equacy; vocabulary a supervisor will recognise (“climate Minsky moment” is common in policy writing). Risk\n\nInvites the question whether the thesis is You lose the explicit story of how fragility a Minsky model or a regression on bank builds up before a shock; you would need data. Adds a heterodox strand to an al- another way to motivate dynamics. ready mixed framework.\n\nWhat to do\n\nKeep it as motivation only; do not claim Cite Minsky in one line as related work to test the two theorems. and move on.\n\nTable 5.3. Lean Minsky versus no Minsky (an input to your decision, not a recommendation).\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ5.1 Define hedge, speculative and Ponzi finance.\nQ5.2 State the two theorems of the financial instability hypothesis.\nQ5.3 What does the Kalecki–Levy profit equation say in the skeletal model?\nExplain\nQ5.4 Why does Minsky say that whether debts are validated depends on investment?\nQ5.5 In what sense is the hypothesis “endogenous”? What would an exogenous theory say instead?\nQ5.6 Why does he say banks are “merchants of debt” and why does that matter for the quantity theory? Apply\nQ5.7 A firm has debt of Rs 200 at 8%. Its operating cash is Rs 20. Classify it, and say what changes if the interest rate rises to 12%.\nQ5.8 Take a sector exposed to a transition shock (for example thermal power). Describe how the shock could move firms between postures and what the bank sees first. Viva\nQ5.9 “Minsky needs no uncertainty: debt alone creates fragility.” Respond using Keynes (Chapter 4).\nQ5.10 “Your thesis uses regression on bank panel data. Minsky is a theory of cycles. How do"}, {"pdf": 70, "printed": 43, "ch": 5, "text": "they connect?” Give a defensible answer, or say that they do not and explain how you would frame the thesis.\n\nAnswer sketches\nA5.1 Hedge: cash flow covers all payments, principal included. Speculative: covers interest but not principal; must roll over. Ponzi: covers neither; borrows or sells assets to pay interest.\nA5.2 (1) There are financing regimes in which the economy is stable and regimes in which it is unstable. (2) Over prolonged prosperity it moves from stable to unstable regimes.\nA5.3 Aggregate profits equal aggregate investment in each period (plus the government deficit in the richer version).\nA5.4 Profits come from investment, so firms can meet their promises only if investment (their own and others’) continues; and investment continues because it is expected to.\nA5.5 The instability comes from the system’s own financing behaviour in good times, not from outside shocks. An exogenous theory would say a shock (oil price, policy mistake) hits an otherwise stable system.\nA5.6 Banks innovate in assets and liabilities for profit, so there is no stable “money” item with constant velocity; the quantity theory’s presupposition fails.\nA5.7 Interest 16 (8% of 200), cash 20: covers interest, not principal, so speculative. At 12%, interest is 24 > 20, so it becomes Ponzi.\nA5.8 Open answer. A defensible one: a higher carbon cost or lower demand cuts operating cash; firms with high debt service move from hedge to speculative; the bank sees falling interest-coverage and rollover difficulty first.\nA5.9 Minsky does lean on Keynes for the role of expectations: debt is validated only if profit expectations hold, and Keynes explains why those expectations are fragile. Without uncertainty one could argue that lenders would price the fragility in.\nA5.10 One defensible answer: the regression estimates how bank capital behaves in relation to exposure to risky sectors, and Minsky provides a motivation for why such exposure matters. Another honest answer: the two are not formally connected, and the thesis uses Minsky only as background. Do not claim more than you test."}, {"pdf": 71, "printed": 44, "ch": 6, "text": "CHAPTER 6\n\nPhase 1 Synthesis, Lecture Outline and\nTest\n6.1 Four readings side by side\nKnight VII\n\nKnight VIII\n\nKeynes 1937\n\nMinsky 1992\n\nMain question What is risk, what is How do societies Why do output and Why does finance uncertainty? meet uncertainty? jobs fluctuate? become fragile? Core idea\n\nGroups of cases Consolidation and Conventions\nare Debt commitments\nmake\nprobability; specialisation; insur- flimsy; money is a need profits; stabilunique cases do not. ers cannot insure barometer of dis- ity breeds fragility. judgment. trust.\n\nRole of uncer- Defines it.\ntainty\nOutput it ex- Profit;\nthe\nplains\ntrepreneur.\nLevel\n\nPhilosophy.\n\nExplains\ntions.\n\ninstitu- Explains\ninterest Assumed in the\nand investment.\nbackground (expectations of profit).\n\nen- The firm, the corpo- Investment,\nration, speculation. ployment.\nInstitutions.\n\nunem- Financial cycles.\n\nMacro-monetary.\n\nBalance-sheet\nnamics.\n\ndy-\n\nTable 6.1. The four Phase 1 readings compared.\n\nThe readings fit as a stack. Knight tells you what kind of ignorance is at stake. Keynes tells you what people do about it and why that makes investment unstable. Minsky tells you what happens to balance sheets when borrowing is based on that unstable expectation. The thesis, at its most ambitious, would sit at the top of the stack: how does a bank’s capital adequacy respond when climate transition adds a new source of unmeasurable uncertainty to the debts it holds?\n\n6.2 What Phase 1 gives the thesis\n1. A way to say why climate scenarios, not forecasts. Knight and Keynes: some\nfuture matters have no calculable probability.\n2. A precise reason why diversification may not protect. Correlation and common shocks (Figure 3.2); Keynes’s point that pessimism and liquidity preference rise together.\n3. A theory of who bears what. Knight on specialisation and moral hazard, with the question of who bears Type 3 uncertainty in the Indian system (state, banks, markets)."}, {"pdf": 72, "printed": 45, "ch": 6, "text": "4. A justification for treating information as part of the system. Knight on information as a commodity; disclosure regimes as a response.\n\n6.3 Section 6.3: the Minsky-dependent part\nSkip if you drop Minsky.\nIf you keep a lean Minsky, the thesis uses it for exactly one job: to motivate why a transition shock can move borrowers from covering their debts to relying on refinancing, and so affect bank capital. A suitable way to state this in a sentence is: “Following Minsky (1992), a shock that lowers expected cash flows moves borrowers along the hedge–speculative–Ponzi ordering, and the bank’s capital is exposed through that movement.” Do not claim to test the two theorems.\n\n6.4 Framing note: Post-Keynesian ideas and neoclassical tools\nYour own earlier notes recorded that combining a Post-Keynesian (PK) background with neoclassical-style empirical methods is something an examiner may press. One framing, offered as a suggestion, is to separate three levels and say which does which job: Level\n\nWhat you use it for\n\nWhat you do not claim\n\nMotivation\n(PK: Why risk cannot be reduced to a single That the econometrics tests Keynes. Knight, Keynes) forecast; why scenarios. Measurement (econo- Estimating how capital adequacy re- That the estimates reveal structural pametric) lates to exposure and to other bank rameters free of conventions. characteristics. Interpretation\n\nReading results as conditional on ob- That results forecast unmeasurable\nserved periods and stated scenarios.\nevents.\n\nTable 6.2. A suggested separation of levels for the thesis.\n\nIf this wording does not fit your supervisor’s view, change it; the principle is to be explicit about what each tradition is doing.\n\n6.5 A 90-minute lecture on Phase 1\n6.6 Phase 1 test\nAllow 90 minutes and attempt without the notes. The answers follow.\nPart A: short answers (2 marks each)\nQ6.1 Give Knight’s operational test of risk versus uncertainty.\nQ6.2 Name Keynes’s three techniques.\nQ6.3 Why can’t an outside insurer insure an entrepreneur’s decision?"}, {"pdf": 73, "printed": 46, "ch": 6, "text": "Minutes\n\nSegment\n\nContent\n\nBoard / slide\n\n0–10\n\nHook\n\nTwo bets: a fair die and “will India’s carbon Two columns on\nprice be above X in 2040?” What is different? the board.\n\n10–30\n\nKnight: risk vs uncer- Three types, the grouping test, the homogene- Figure 2.1; tainty ity trap. ble 2.2.\n\n30–45\n\nKnight: meeting un- Consolidation, specialisation, moral hazard.\ncertainty\n\n45–65\n\nKeynes: conventions Three techniques, barometer of distrust, chain Figure 4.1. and liquidity to investment.\n\n65–75\n\nMultiplier\n\nNine-tenths example, formula 1/(1 − c).\n\nFigure 4.2.\n\n75–85\n\nThesis link\n\nScenarios, correlation, who bears uncertainty.\n\nOne slide.\n\n85–90\n\nClose\n\nOne question for the room: “What would count –\nas evidence that something is Knightian?”\n\nTa-\n\nFigure 3.2 (independent vs common shock).\n\nTable 6.3. Lecture outline for Phase 1 (without Minsky). If you keep Minsky, add 10 minutes after Keynes with Table 5.1 and Figure 5.1, and cut the multiplier segment to five minutes.\n\nQ6.4 What does the rate of interest measure in Keynes?\nQ6.5 Define a Ponzi unit in Minsky’s sense.\nPart B: explain (8 marks each)\nQ6.6 Explain, using the formula for the spread of a pool’s loss rate, why diversification fails against a common shock.\nQ6.7 Compare Knight’s and Keynes’s accounts of uncertainty. What does each use it for?\nQ6.8 Explain why Keynes argues that pessimism about yields and liquidity preference reinforce each other. Part C: essays (20 marks each, choose one)\nQ6.9 “Climate transition risk is Knightian uncertainty, so capital adequacy rules built on VaR are misconceived.” Discuss.\nQ6.10 Evaluate the claim that the thesis can use Keynes’s theory of uncertainty without committing to a Post-Keynesian methodology.\nQ6.11 (If Minsky is kept.) Assess the usefulness of the hedge–speculative–Ponzi classification for a bank supervisor facing transition risk.\n\nAnswer sketches\nA6.1 Can you form a group of similar cases and know the frequency of outcomes? Yes: risk. No, because the case is too unique: uncertainty.\nA6.2 Present as a guide to the future; prices embody correct opinion; follow the majority.\nA6.3 The risk lies in the decision-maker’s own judgment, which the insurer cannot classify or"}, {"pdf": 74, "printed": 47, "ch": 6, "text": "control; insuring it would remove the incentive to judge well (moral hazard).\nA6.4 The premium needed to induce people not to hold wealth as money; a barometer of distrust of our calculations and conventions.\nA6.5 A unit whose operating cash flow covers neither principal nor interest; it must borrow or sell assets to pay interest, lowering its margin of safety.\nA6.6 For loss probability p, pairwise correlation ρ and n loans, the spread of the loss rate is p(1 − p) ρ + (1 − ρ)/n. As n → ∞ the second term vanishes but the ρ term remains, so a floor of p(1 − p)ρ is left. Transition shocks raise ρ within sectors.\nA6.7 Common: both separate calculable from non-calculable. Knight: unique cases defeat grouping, which explains profit and institutions. Keynes: no scientific basis for probability on long-run matters, which explains conventions, liquidity preference and unstable investment.\nA6.8 The same circumstances that make yield expectations pessimistic raise the desire for liquidity; the offsetting force (falling activity releasing cash) is slow and uncertain.\nA6.9 A strong answer separates (a) which parts of transition risk are measurable (physical hazard frequencies, current exposures) from which are not (policy timing); (b) that VaR/CVaR can be read as scenario-conditional stress measures and not as claims about unconditional probability; (c) that Knight allows some regularity in grouped estimates and that the point is humility about the number, not abandonment of measurement.\nA6.10 A strong answer distinguishes the concept (non-calculable knowledge) from the method (PK macro modelling); argues the thesis uses the first for motivation and standard econometrics for estimation, and states clearly what each can support (Table 6.2).\nA6.11 Strengths: simple, based on cash flow, can be built from interest coverage. Limits: thresholds not specified in the paper; bank data may not show rollover; classification is firm-level while capital is bank-level; the framework does not itself predict the timing of a transition shock."}], "cards": [{"id": "1.1", "ch": 1, "lv": "Recall", "q": "In one sentence each, say what Knight, Keynes and Minsky contribute to the chain of thought described in Section 1.1.", "a": "Knight: the future cannot be priced for decisions that are unique; the economy’s institutions are shaped by that. Keynes: people therefore rely on conventions, which makes money demand, interest rates and investment unstable. Minsky: debt commitments turn that instability into a self-reinforcing cycle."}, {"id": "1.2", "ch": 1, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Name two ways in which a pool of loans can behave differently from a pool of independent coin tosses.", "a": "Correlation (loans move together under a common shock) and dependence on unknown or changing probabilities (the default rate itself is not known and may change). Either one leaves a risk that pooling cannot remove."}, {"id": "1.3", "ch": 1, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does the law of large numbers make an insurer’s business predictable but a single firm’s decision not?", "a": "The insurer faces many alike cases and can estimate frequencies, so the law of large numbers works. A firm facing a unique choice has no group of similar cases to average over."}, {"id": "1.4", "ch": 1, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Write down three decisions a public sector bank makes that you think have a usable history of similar cases, and three that do not. Keep the list; you will revisit it after Chapter 3.", "a": "Expect a mix. For example, with history: retail loan delinquencies, working-capital loans to stable firms. Without: lending to a new green-hydrogen venture, or the effect of a future domestic carbon price."}, {"id": "1.5", "ch": 1, "lv": "Viva", "q": "An examiner asks: “You say climate risk is uncertain, not risky. Then why does your thesis compute probabilities and CVaR?” Draft a two-sentence answer now, and compare it with your answer after Chapter 2.", "a": "A strong answer separates the question (“what is the true probability?”) from the use (“what loss do we hold capital for?”). Scenarios and CVaR are used as stress-testing devices conditional on stated stories, not as claims about objective probability."}, {"id": "2.1", "ch": 2, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Why does Knight say that change, taken alone, does not produce profit?", "a": "Because if changes followed known laws they would be foreseen and built into costs. Profit arises from the divergence between anticipated and actual outcomes, which comes from ignorance of the future, not from change itself."}, {"id": "2.2", "ch": 2, "lv": "Recall", "q": "List Knight’s four sources of error (Table 2.1) without looking.", "a": "Failure to perceive the present accurately and completely; failure to infer the future dependably from the present; failure to know the consequences of our own actions accurately; failure to execute actions in exactly the form intended."}, {"id": "2.3", "ch": 2, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What is the difference between an a priori and a statistical probability? Give one example of each.", "a": "A priori: calculated from the structure of the problem (a perfect die, one chance in six). Statistical: obtained by counting experience (the proportion of buildings that burn in a region). The test is whether you could, in principle, compute it without observation."}, {"id": "2.4", "ch": 2, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What does Knight mean by a “third type” of probability? Why does he say the name is misleading?", "a": "Estimates: judgments for which there is no valid basis of classifying instances. He says that calling it “probability” is misleading because it lacks the objective, measurable character of the first two, but he keeps the name because it is entrenched, and insists on the differences."}, {"id": "2.5", "ch": 2, "lv": "Explain", "q": "The Philippines soldiers example shows a trap in statistical reasoning. State the trap and explain how it connects to Knight’s paradox about homogeneity.", "a": "The trap: comparing groups with different make-ups (soldiers are young and healthy; the general population is not). Knight’s paradox: with perfectly homogeneous groups there would be no probability at all, only uniformity, so probability reasoning needs imperfect homogeneity, and thus classification is always imperfect."}, {"id": "2.6", "ch": 2, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does Knight say that the formation of an estimate and the estimation of its value are two separate exercises of judgment? What would be lost if they were treated as one?", "a": "They can differ and err independently: you might form a good estimate and be unsure of it, or a poor estimate and feel confident. Action follows confidence as much as the estimate. Treating them as one would hide the fact that confidence is what drives conduct, and that it can be unrelated to accuracy."}, {"id": "2.7", "ch": 2, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Knight says every instance can be grouped with some others if the standard of similarity is loose enough. Why does this not collapse his distinction between risk and uncertainty?", "a": "Because the groups it forms for estimates are loose, give no measured proportions, and are not a basis for prediction in a single case. They give “some tendency toward regularity” in aggregate and after the fact, not a computable distribution beforehand. Knight treats the difference as one of degree but does not abolish the distinction."}, {"id": "2.8", "ch": 2, "lv": "Apply", "q": "For each of the following, say whether it is closest to Type 1, Type 2 or Type 3, and say what would change your classification: (a) the chance that a given Indian corporate borrower defaults next year; (b) the chance that India adopts an economy-wide carbon tax by 2030; (c) the chance of a cyclone hitting a given coastal district in a year; (d) the effect of a carbon tax on the profits of a cement producer.", "a": "(a) Mostly Type 2 where there is a long portfolio history, but drifting to Type 3 for a new or unusual borrower. (b) Type 3: unique political decision. (c) Type 2: long hazard records, though climate change may shift the frequencies. (d) Type 3 in the aggregate (policy design and timing unknown) but with Type 2 components (observed cost pass-through in past price changes)."}, {"id": "2.9", "ch": 2, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Take Table 2.1 and, for each of the four sources of error, give one example from public sector bank lending to a carbon-intensive sector.", "a": "Examples: (1) accounts show assets but not a plant’s real emissions exposure; (2) projecting past sector default rates forward while policy changes the environment; (3) a lending restriction pushes borrowers to informal finance and the effect is unknown; (4) a credit-policy circular is applied inconsistently by branches."}, {"id": "2.10", "ch": 2, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“If climate risk is Knightian uncertainty, probabilities are meaningless, so why does your thesis use CVaR?” Give a three-sentence answer that does not abandon either Knight or CVaR.", "a": "CVaR is not used to measure the unmeasurable. It is a stress measure conditional on stated scenarios: given this story, how large is the loss in the worst tail? The scenarios are openly assumptions, which is exactly Knight’s point that for Type 3 situations we have judgments, not distributions."}, {"id": "2.11", "ch": 2, "lv": "Viva", "q": "A critic says: “Knight’s distinction is outdated, because Bayesian decision theory treats all uncertainty as subjective probability.” Give one way you might respond using only what Knight says in this chapter.", "a": "Knight accepts that people carry subjective probabilities, which is why he calls the third type “probability” too. His point is that such judgments cannot be checked against a group of cases, so they cannot be pooled or insured, and a theory of profit and enterprise needs the difference. A Bayesian reply tells us how to act; it does not make the estimate objective."}, {"id": "3.1", "ch": 3, "lv": "Recall", "q": "State Knight’s definitions of risk and uncertainty and the one-question test that separates them.", "a": "Risk: measurable uncertainty; the distribution of outcomes in a group of instances is known, by calculation or by past statistics. Uncertainty: unmeasurable; the situation is too unique to form a group. Test: can you form a meaningful group of similar cases and know the frequency of each outcome?"}, {"id": "3.2", "ch": 3, "lv": "Recall", "q": "List his methods for meeting uncertainty in order. Which two does he call fundamental?", "a": "Consolidation, specialization, control of the future, better prediction, (possibly) diffusion, choice of less uncertain activities. The first two are fundamental."}, {"id": "3.3", "ch": 3, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Name Knight’s five variables on which people differ in relation to uncertainty.", "a": "Capacity to form correct judgments; capacity to judge means; capacity to execute; degree of confidence (largely independent of accuracy); conative attitude (feeling towards uncertainty itself)."}, {"id": "3.4", "ch": 3, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Why, according to Knight, can an outside insurer not insure a business decision?", "a": "Because the relevant uncertainty lies in the decision-maker’s own judgment: the insurer cannot classify or control the “instance”, and the insured would have less reason to take care (moral hazard). Estimates also only offset after the fact."}, {"id": "3.5", "ch": 3, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does Knight say that for a single decision there is no difference for conduct between risk and uncertainty, and why does the difference nevertheless matter for the economy as a whole?", "a": "For one decision the person acts on a felt probability whether it is objective or not. The difference matters when you aggregate: measurable risk can be removed by grouping, uncertainty cannot be removed by an outside party. It therefore decides who bears what."}, {"id": "3.6", "ch": 3, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Explain how “production for a market” is a response to uncertainty, in Knight’s argument.", "a": "The consumer cannot foresee his own wants, but a producer facing a multitude can foresee their aggregate wants better than each can foresee his own, by the law of large numbers. Producing ahead for the market is therefore a consolidation."}, {"id": "3.7", "ch": 3, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Explain why limited liability is not, for Knight, the main way in which the corporation reduces risk.", "a": "Because limited liability only moves losses beyond invested capital from owners to creditors. The real reduction comes from combining many ventures under unified control (consolidation) plus publicity and diffusion across shareholders."}, {"id": "3.8", "ch": 3, "lv": "Explain", "q": "How does Knight distinguish the insurer from the speculator? Why does the distinction lead him to say that uncertainty-bearing and management are inseparable?", "a": "The insurer’s risk is lower because he takes many cases, not because he knows more about each. The speculator or entrepreneur substitutes his own judgment, which if better lowers the individual risk too, so he is also doing better management. The two roles cannot be separated for the person ultimately responsible."}, {"id": "3.9", "ch": 3, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Use Figure 3.2 to explain, in four or five sentences, why a bank cannot diversify away transition risk by lending to more borrowers within the same carbon-intensive sector.", "a": "Loans in the same sector or region face the same policy or physical shock, so their outcomes move together. The pool’s loss spread falls with size only for the independent part; the common-shock part leaves a floor. Adding more borrowers in the same sector adds numbers but not independence."}, {"id": "3.10", "ch": 3, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Place the following on Knight’s spectrum of insurability (Table 3.3) and say why: (a) crop insurance in a monsoon-dependent district; (b) insurance against loss from a future domestic carbon price; (c) a credit-guarantee scheme for small borrowers.", "a": "(a) Partly insurable: long district-level weather history gives statistical probabilities, but with correlated losses across the district. (b) Closer to the uninsurable end: unique policy event, no group of cases, and moral hazard (firms can lobby or time investment). (c) Insurable to the extent that default history for small borrowers allows classification, though losses correlate in downturns."}, {"id": "3.11", "ch": 3, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Who, in the Indian financial system, ends up bearing the part of transition uncertainty that cannot be pooled or insured? Give your answer as a chain of specialisation in Knight’s sense.", "a": "A defensible chain: lenders pass uncertainty to the banks; the banks bear it as claims on borrowers; public sector owners and the state bear it through recapitalisation; ultimately taxpayers. Compare with private actors (equity holders, green investors) who specialise voluntarily. The answer is yours to defend; the point is the ordering and what each party can pool."}, {"id": "3.12", "ch": 3, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“Knight’s framework says uncertainty can be reduced inside firms by scale. So large Indian banks, with huge loan books, should be well placed to handle climate uncertainty. Why is that wrong?” (Use Figure 3.2.)", "a": "Because a bigger loan book consolidates independent errors, not shared ones. Transition and physical shocks correlate across borrowers in a sector or region, so size alone leaves a floor of risk. Scale also does not help with the unknowable timing of policy."}, {"id": "3.13", "ch": 3, "lv": "Viva", "q": "A supervisor says: “If information is a commodity produced because of uncertainty, then mandatory disclosure just produces more noise.” Give an answer that uses Knight’s distinction between information, persuasion and guidance.", "a": "Knight separates information that reduces ignorance, persuasion that merely moves belief, and guidance that prescribes. Disclosure aimed at reducing ignorance (standardised metrics that allow classification) is information; the risk is that disclosure turns into persuasion (marketing). So the design of disclosure matters, not just its volume."}, {"id": "4.1", "ch": 4, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What is the meaning of “uncertain” in Keynes’s 1937 paper? Give his examples on both sides of the line.", "a": "Not merely “probable” as against “certain”: uncertain means no scientific basis for any calculable probability. Calculable side: roulette, Victory bond, life expectancy, the weather. Uncertain side: a European war, copper price and interest rate twenty years on, obsolescence of an invention, the position of wealth-owners in 1970."}, {"id": "4.2", "ch": 4, "lv": "Recall", "q": "State the three techniques by which people cope with uncertainty.", "a": "Assume the present is a good guide to the future; assume current prices embody correct opinion; conform to the majority (convention)."}, {"id": "4.3", "ch": 4, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What, according to Keynes, does the rate of interest measure?", "a": "The premium needed to induce people to hold wealth in some form other than hoarded money; it is a barometer of distrust of our own calculations and conventions."}, {"id": "4.4", "ch": 4, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What is the “psychological law” behind the multiplier?", "a": "When income rises, consumption rises but by less, so the increase is divided between spending and saving."}, {"id": "4.5", "ch": 4, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does Keynes say that the quantity of money hoarded is the wrong thing to look at when studying liquidity preference?", "a": "Because a rise in liquidity preference shows itself mainly as a higher interest rate (a fall in security prices), not as larger hoards; the supply of hoardable cash is limited. The premium is what matters."}, {"id": "4.6", "ch": 4, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Explain, in words, why the two sources of instability in investment do not offset each other.", "a": "The same circumstances that make people pessimistic about future yields also raise the urge to hold money, so the two reinforce each other. The only offsetting force (lower activity releasing cash) arrives later and uncertainly."}, {"id": "4.7", "ch": 4, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Explain how the multiplier follows from the propensity to consume. Compute the multiplier for c = 0.8.", "a": "If c is the share of income consumed, income is investment divided by (1 − c), so a rise in investment multiplies. For c = 0.8, 1/(1 − 0.8) = 5."}, {"id": "4.8", "ch": 4, "lv": "Explain", "q": "In what sense is classical theory “one of these pretty, polite techniques”?", "a": "It handles the present by abstracting from the fact that we know little about the future, just as ordinary behaviour does by conventions; it mirrors the market-place’s techniques instead of examining them."}, {"id": "4.9", "ch": 4, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Choose a recent change in climate policy or disclosure that affected bank-loan or equity valuations in India. Describe it using Keynes’s three techniques and the idea of a “new conventional basis of valuation”.", "a": "Open answer. A good one identifies who extrapolated the old policy (1), who relied on prices (2), who followed peers (3), and what triggered revaluation."}, {"id": "4.10", "ch": 4, "lv": "Apply", "q": "A bank sets its capital buffer using a single central forecast of the carbon price. Criticise this using Keynes’s examples of uncertain matters.", "a": "A single forecast assumes a calculable probability for something Keynes puts on the uncertain side (the interest rate twenty years ahead, obsolescence of technology). Scenarios or ranges are the honest response."}, {"id": "4.11", "ch": 4, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Suppose banks’ liquidity preference rises sharply after a shock. Following Keynes, what happens to the rate of interest, asset prices and investment, and what would a central bank have to do to offset it?", "a": "The interest rate rises and asset prices fall, so investment falls. The central bank would have to supply liquidity (increase the quantity of money available) so the premium needed falls."}, {"id": "4.12", "ch": 4, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“Knight and Keynes say the same thing, so citing both is redundant.” Respond using", "a": "The core distinction is shared, but Knight is explaining profit and the firm and proposes institutions that tame uncertainty; Keynes is explaining liquidity preference, interest and unstable investment and proposes that conventions are fragile. Different downstream use justifies citing both."}, {"id": "4.13", "ch": 4, "lv": "Viva", "q": "An examiner says: “Keynesian uncertainty means you cannot model anything, yet your thesis is an econometric model.” How do you respond?", "a": "Keynes does not say nothing can be modelled; he says that predictions of the long-run future are not calculable, and that models should be treated as conventions with limited reliability. The thesis can use econometrics to estimate historical relationships and scenarios for conditional what-if analysis while being explicit that it does not forecast the unknowable."}, {"id": "5.1", "ch": 5, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Define hedge, speculative and Ponzi finance.", "a": "Hedge: cash flow covers all payments, principal included. Speculative: covers interest but not principal; must roll over. Ponzi: covers neither; borrows or sells assets to pay interest."}, {"id": "5.2", "ch": 5, "lv": "Recall", "q": "State the two theorems of the financial instability hypothesis.", "a": "(1) There are financing regimes in which the economy is stable and regimes in which it is unstable. (2) Over prolonged prosperity it moves from stable to unstable regimes."}, {"id": "5.3", "ch": 5, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What does the Kalecki–Levy profit equation say in the skeletal model?", "a": "Aggregate profits equal aggregate investment in each period (plus the government deficit in the richer version)."}, {"id": "5.4", "ch": 5, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does Minsky say that whether debts are validated depends on investment?", "a": "Profits come from investment, so firms can meet their promises only if investment (their own and others’) continues; and investment continues because it is expected to."}, {"id": "5.5", "ch": 5, "lv": "Explain", "q": "In what sense is the hypothesis “endogenous”? What would an exogenous theory say instead?", "a": "The instability comes from the system’s own financing behaviour in good times, not from outside shocks. An exogenous theory would say a shock (oil price, policy mistake) hits an otherwise stable system."}, {"id": "5.6", "ch": 5, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does he say banks are “merchants of debt” and why does that matter for the quantity theory?", "a": "Banks innovate in assets and liabilities for profit, so there is no stable “money” item with constant velocity; the quantity theory’s presupposition fails."}, {"id": "5.7", "ch": 5, "lv": "Apply", "q": "A firm has debt of Rs 200 at 8%. Its operating cash is Rs 20. Classify it, and say what changes if the interest rate rises to 12%.", "a": "Interest 16 (8% of 200), cash 20: covers interest, not principal, so speculative. At 12%, interest is 24 > 20, so it becomes Ponzi."}, {"id": "5.8", "ch": 5, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Take a sector exposed to a transition shock (for example thermal power). Describe how the shock could move firms between postures and what the bank sees first.", "a": "Open answer. A defensible one: a higher carbon cost or lower demand cuts operating cash; firms with high debt service move from hedge to speculative; the bank sees falling interest-coverage and rollover difficulty first."}, {"id": "5.9", "ch": 5, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“Minsky needs no uncertainty: debt alone creates fragility.” Respond using Keynes (Chapter 4).", "a": "Minsky does lean on Keynes for the role of expectations: debt is validated only if profit expectations hold, and Keynes explains why those expectations are fragile. Without uncertainty one could argue that lenders would price the fragility in."}, {"id": "5.10", "ch": 5, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“Your thesis uses regression on bank panel data. Minsky is a theory of cycles. How do they connect?” Give a defensible answer, or say that they do not and explain how you would frame the thesis.", "a": "One defensible answer: the regression estimates how bank capital behaves in relation to exposure to risky sectors, and Minsky provides a motivation for why such exposure matters. Another honest answer: the two are not formally connected, and the thesis uses Minsky only as background. Do not claim more than you test."}, {"id": "6.1", "ch": 6, "lv": "Test", "q": "Give Knight’s operational test of risk versus uncertainty.", "a": "Can you form a group of similar cases and know the frequency of outcomes? Yes: risk. No, because the case is too unique: uncertainty."}, {"id": "6.2", "ch": 6, "lv": "Test", "q": "Name Keynes’s three techniques.", "a": "Present as a guide to the future; prices embody correct opinion; follow the majority."}, {"id": "6.3", "ch": 6, "lv": "Test", "q": "Why can’t an outside insurer insure an entrepreneur’s decision?", "a": "The risk lies in the decision-maker’s own judgment, which the insurer cannot classify or control; insuring it would remove the incentive to judge well (moral hazard)."}, {"id": "6.4", "ch": 6, "lv": "Test", "q": "What does the rate of interest measure in Keynes?", "a": "The premium needed to induce people not to hold wealth as money; a barometer of distrust of our calculations and conventions."}, {"id": "6.5", "ch": 6, "lv": "Test", "q": "Define a Ponzi unit in Minsky’s sense. Part B: explain (8 marks each)", "a": "A unit whose operating cash flow covers neither principal nor interest; it must borrow or sell assets to pay interest, lowering its margin of safety."}, {"id": "6.6", "ch": 6, "lv": "Test", "q": "Explain, using the formula for the spread of a pool’s loss rate, why diversification fails against a common shock.", "a": "For loss probability p, pairwise correlation ρ and n loans, the spread of the loss rate is p p p(1 − p) ρ + (1 − ρ)/n. As n → ∞ the second term vanishes but the ρ term remains, p so a floor of p(1 − p)ρ is left. Transition shocks raise ρ within sectors."}, {"id": "6.7", "ch": 6, "lv": "Test", "q": "Compare Knight’s and Keynes’s accounts of uncertainty. What does each use it for?", "a": "Common: both separate calculable from non-calculable. Knight: unique cases defeat grouping, which explains profit and institutions. Keynes: no scientific basis for probability on long-run matters, which explains conventions, liquidity preference and unstable investment."}, {"id": "6.8", "ch": 6, "lv": "Test", "q": "Explain why Keynes argues that pessimism about yields and liquidity preference reinforce each other. Part C: essays (20 marks each, choose one)", "a": "The same circumstances that make yield expectations pessimistic raise the desire for liquidity; the offsetting force (falling activity releasing cash) is slow and uncertain."}, {"id": "6.9", "ch": 6, "lv": "Test", "q": "“Climate transition risk is Knightian uncertainty, so capital adequacy rules built on VaR are misconceived.” Discuss.", "a": "A strong answer separates (a) which parts of transition risk are measurable (physical hazard frequencies, current exposures) from which are not (policy timing); (b) that VaR/CVaR can be read as scenario-conditional stress measures and not as claims about unconditional probability; (c) that Knight allows some regularity in grouped estimates and that the point is humility about the number, not abandonment of measurement."}, {"id": "6.10", "ch": 6, "lv": "Test", "q": "Evaluate the claim that the thesis can use Keynes’s theory of uncertainty without committing to a Post-Keynesian methodology.", "a": "A strong answer distinguishes the concept (non-calculable knowledge) from the method (PK macro modelling); argues the thesis uses the first for motivation and standard econometrics for estimation, and states clearly what each can support (Table 6.2)."}, {"id": "6.11", "ch": 6, "lv": "Test", "q": "(If Minsky is kept.) Assess the usefulness of the hedge–speculative–Ponzi classification for a bank supervisor facing transition risk.", "a": "Strengths: simple, based on cash flow, can be built from interest coverage. Limits: thresholds not specified in the paper; bank data may not show rollover; classification is firm-level while capital is bank-level; the framework does not itself predict the timing of a transition shock."}]};